We have:
\begin{align*}
(ab+c)^2=0&\Rightarrow abc+cab=0
(ca+b)^2=0&\Rightarrow cab+bca=0
(bc+a)^2=0&\Rightarrow bca+abc=0\end{align*}
Adding the first and the last implies  $abc+abc+cab+bca=0$ , giving the result.  $\square$

Old but very, very fun. I do not believe you can do better than the constant  $c=\pi$ . I am going through PFTB these days, and I think the version of this problem is much harder in that book because it does not state part (a). It is still not so difficult. **<span style="color:#E69138">Part (a):</span>** This is very quick. I think it's just there as motivation for how to come up with the next part.**Claim:**  We have \[\sum_{j=1}^n x_j^2 \leq \left(\sum^n_{i=1}\frac{x_i}{\sqrt{i}}\right)^2.\]
The proof is not hard. I will sketch it <details><summary>here.</summary>We have  \begin{align*}
\left(\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{x_i}{\sqrt{i}}\right)^2 
&= \sum_{j=1}^n \sum_{k=1}^n \frac{x_j x_k}{\sqrt{j}\sqrt{k}} 
&\ge \sum_{j=1}^n \frac{x_j}{\sqrt{j}} \sum_{k=1}^j \frac{x_k}{\sqrt{k}} 
&\ge \sum_{j=1}^n \frac{x_j}{\sqrt{j}} \cdot (j) \cdot \frac{x_j}{\sqrt{j}} 
&= \sum_{j=1}^n x_j^2 
\end{align*} as desired.</details>**<span style="color:#E69138">Part (b):</span>** This part is much more fun. I want to note that the problem is *assuming* that  $\sum_{n \ge 1}x_n$  converges in the problem statement. Otherwise, it would not make sense. Moreover, we have  $x_i >0$ , so we can assume absolute convergence and rearrange terms however we like. Reindexing the inequality from part (a), we have \[\sum^{\infty}_{k=1}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}\sqrt{\sum^{\infty}_{i=k}x_i^2} \le \sum_{k=1}^\infty\sum_{i=k}^\infty\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}\frac{x_i}{\sqrt{i-k+1}}.\] Swapping the order of summation implies \[\sum_{k=1}^\infty\sum_{i=k}^\infty\frac{x_i}{\sqrt{k}}\frac{1}{\sqrt{i-k+1}} = \sum_{i=1}^\infty x_i \sum_{k=1}^i\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}\sqrt{i-k+1}} \le \sum_{i=1}^\infty x_i \int_0^{i+1} \frac{1}{\sqrt{t}\sqrt{i-t+1}} \, \mathrm{d}t.\] Some routine asymptotics imply \[
\int_0^{i+1} \frac{1}{\sqrt{t} \sqrt{i - t + 1}} \, \mathrm{d}t 
\le 
\int_0^1 \frac{1}{\sqrt{x(1 - x)}} \, \mathrm{d}x 
= \frac{\Gamma\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^2}{\Gamma(1)} 
= \pi.
\]
Together this implies \[\boxed{\sum^{\infty}_{k=1}\frac{1}{\sqrt{k}}\sqrt{\sum^{\infty}_{i=k}x_i^2} \le \pi\sum_{i \ge 1}x_i}\] so the problem is solved.

EDIT: I guess you can achieve  $C= \frac{\pi}{2}$  as well so my hope that this solution was optimal is not true. I guess my bound still solves the problem, though.  :dry:

