Define the generating function  $A(x) = \sum_{k \ge 0} a_kx^k$ . Then
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k \ge 0} f(k) x^k &= \sum_{k \ge 0} \sum_{n \ge 0} \binom{k-1}{n,n,k-1-2n} 
&= \sum_{n \ge 0} \binom{2n}{n} \sum_{k \ge 0} \binom{k}{2n} x^k 
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\frac{4x^2}{(1-x)^2}}} 
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-3x}} \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+x}} 
&= \left(\sum_{n \ge 0} \binom{2n}{n} \left(\frac 34 x\right)^n\right) \cdot \left(\sum_{n \ge 0} \binom{2n}{n} \left(-\frac 14 x\right)^n\right).
\end{align*}

The only  $x^p$  term in the expansion of this expression which isn't a multiple of  $p$  is
\[\binom{p-1}{(p-1)/2} \cdot \left(-\frac{3}{16}\right)^{(p-1)/2} \equiv (-3)^{(p-1)/2} \pmod p.\]

Quick FLT and quadratic reciprocity along with confirming our edge cases  $p=2,3$  gives us our answer
\[
\boxed{f(p) \pmod p \equiv \begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } p \equiv 1 \pmod 3 
-1 & \text{if } p \equiv 2 \pmod 3 
0 & \text{if } p = 3
\end{cases}}
\]

We want the coefficient of  $x^{p-1}$  in \[P(x) = (1+x+x^2)^{p-1}.\] Working in  $\mathbb{F}_p$ ,
\begin{align*}
[x^{p-1}] P(x) &= [x^{p-1}] (1+x+x^2)^{p-1}
&= [x^{p-1}] \left(\tfrac{1-x^3}{1-x}\right)^{p-1}
&= [x^{p-1}] \left(\tfrac{1-x^3}{1-x}\right)^p \cdot \tfrac{1-x}{1-x^3}
&= [x^{p-1}] \tfrac{1-x^{3p}}{1-x^p} \cdot \tfrac{1-x}{1-x^3}
&= [x^{p-1}] \left(1+x^p+x^{2p}\right) \cdot \tfrac{1-x}{1-x^3}
&= [x^{p-1}] \tfrac{1-x}{1-x^3} 
&= [x^{p-1}] (1-x)+(x^3-x^4)+\dots.
\end{align*}
Hence, the required answer is: \[\begin{cases} 0 & \text{if }p \equiv 0 \pmod{3}  1 & \text{if }p \equiv 1 \pmod{3}  p-1 & \text{if }p \equiv 2 \pmod{3}\end{cases}\] so we are done.  $\square$

