where  $x$  is the smallest positive integer not in  $x,$  so we need   $$ 36-x \le 32 \implies x \le 4. $$  If  $4$  is the smallest positive integer not in  $(a_1, \ldots, x_7),$  then we get the singular  $7$ -tuple   $$ (1,2,3,5,6,7,8). $$   If  $5$  is the smallest positive integer not in  $(a_1, \ldots, a_7),$  then we get the singular  $7$ -tuple   $$ (1,2,3,4,6,7,9). $$   If  $6$  is the smallest positive integer not in  $(a_1, \ldots, a_7),$  then we get   $$ (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 10), (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9). $$   Finally, if  $7$  is the smallest positive integer not in  $(a_1, \ldots, a_7),$  then we get   $$ (1,2,3,4,5,6,11). $$   Clearly, it is not possible for  $a_7$  to be  $11$  because then a rectangle would need to have  $22$  squares and neither a  $1 \times 22$  nor a  $2 \times 11$  rectangle would fit on a  $8 \times 8$  chessboard. However, the other four  $7$ -tuples do indeed work, as evidenced by the following tilings:
