&+ 43 \omega^{10} + 47 \omega^{209} + 51\omega^{408} + 55 \omega^{607} + 59 \omega^{806}  &\vdots 
\end{align*}

Intuitively, we would probably like line segments with "opposite direction" to be as close in length as possible. Therefore, place the line segment with length  $1^2$  opposite to the one with length  $2^2$ , and so on, with  $1989^2$  opposite to  $1990^2$ . 

Set  $\omega = e^{2\pi i / 1990}$ . For there to exist such a  $1990$ -gon, this is equivalent to  $$ 0 = \sum_{k=0}^{994}c_k\omega^k $$  where  $c_k \in \{3, 7, \dots, 3979\}$ . 

This is essentially somewhat similar to our original problem but with a  $995$ -gon. Now, consider the value  $x = 3 + 7\omega^{199} + 11 \omega^{398} + 15 \omega^{597} + 19 \omega^{796}$ . The following sum holds:

\begin{align*}
    x &= 3 + 7\omega^{199} + 11 \omega^{398} + 15 \omega^{597} + 19 \omega^{796} 
    x \cdot \omega^5 &= 3\omega^5 + 7\omega^{204} + 11\omega^{403} + 15 \omega^{602} + 19 \omega^{801} 
     &= x \cdot \omega^5 + (20\omega^5 + 20\omega^{204} + 20\omega^{403} + 20 \omega^{602} + 20\omega^{801}) 
     &= 23\omega^5 + 27\omega^{204} + 31\omega^{403} + 35 \omega^{602} + 39 \omega^{801} 
     x \cdot \omega^{10} &= 43\omega^{10} + 47\omega^{209} + 51\omega^{408} + 55 \omega^{607} + 59 \omega^{806} 
    \dots
\end{align*}

Summing these equations all up, we get our desired RHS, and the LHS equal to  $0$ :
\begin{align*}
x \cdot (1 + \omega^5 + \dots + \omega^{990}) &= \sum_{k=0}^{994} c_k \omega^k \text{    }\{c_k \in \{3, 7, \dots, 3979\}\}
\implies 0 &= \sum_{k=0}^{994}c_k\omega^k \text{    }\{c_k \in \{3, 7, \dots, 3979\}\}
\end{align*}

as desired!

Let  $\omega$  be a primitive 995 root. The problem is equivalent to proving that there exists a permutation  $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_{1990},$  of the first 1990 numbers such that  $\sum_{k = 0}^{1989} k^2\exp\left(k i \pi /995\right) = 0.$  Put  $(2n)^2$  and  $(2n - 1)^2$  on opposite sides. Thus, our problem is equivalent to showing there exists a permutation  $b_i$  of  $0, 1, \ldots, 994$  such that  $\sum_{i = 0}^{994}b_i\omega^i = 0.$  Let  $z = 0 \cdot \omega^0 + 1 \omega^{199} + 2 \omega^{398} + 3 \omega^{597} + 4 \omega^{796}.$  Thus, we have that:
\begin{align*}
	z & = 0 \cdot \omega^0 + 1 \omega^{199} + 2 \omega^{398} + 3 \omega^{597} + 4 \omega^{796} 
	\omega^5z & = 0 \omega^5 + 1 \omega^{199 + 5} + 2 \omega^{398 + 5} + 3 \omega^{597 + 5} + 4 \omega^{796 + 5} 
	& = 5 \omega^5 + 6 \omega^{199 + 5} + 7 \omega^{398 + 5} + 8 \omega^{597 + 5} + 9 \omega^{796 + 5} 
	& \vdots 
	\omega^{990}z & = 0 \omega^{990} + 1 \omega^{194} + 2 \omega^{393} + 3 \omega^{592} + 4 \omega^{791} 
	& = 990 \omega^{990} + 991 \omega^{194} + 992 \omega^{393} + 993 \omega^{592} + 994 \omega^{791}
\end{align*}
By adding all of them together, we are done.

We first note that we may rephrase the problem to show that there exists some permutation of  $a = \{1^2, 2^2, 3^2, \dots, 1990^2\}$  for which \[\sum_{k=1}^{1990} a_k\operatorname{cis}\left(\frac{2\pi ik}{1990}\right) = 0.\] Because of size constraints, we put consecutive squares on opposite sides. Then, we make a new set 
\begin{align*}
b &= \{2^2-1^2, 4^2-3^2, \dots, 1990^2-1989^2\} 
&= \{3, 7, \dots, 3979\}.
\end{align*}
In addition, if we let  $\omega = e^{2i\pi/995}$ , we need to show that there is some permutation of  $b$  where \[\sum_{k=1}^{995} b_k\operatorname{cis}(\omega^k) = 0.\] We note that  $\omega^0+\omega^{199}+\omega^{398}+\omega^{597}+\omega^{796} =  0$ , and using this, we get the construction
\begin{align*}
&3 \omega^0 + 7 \omega^{199} + 11\omega^{398} + 15 \omega^{597} + 19 \omega^{796} 
&+ 23 \omega^5 + 27 \omega^{204} + 31\omega^{403} + 35 \omega^{602} + 39 \omega^{801} 
&+ 43 \omega^{10} + 47 \omega^{209} + 51\omega^{408} + 55 \omega^{607} + 59 \omega^{806}  &\vdots 
&= 0
\end{align*}
as desired.

Let  $t=e^{\frac{2\pi i}{995}}$ ,  $j\in\{1,2,\dots,995\}$ , and  $k\in\{0,1,2\dots,199\}$ . Put the sides with length  $(2j-1)^2$  and  $2j^2$  on opposite ends of the polygon. Then the sum of these pairs of vectors facing opposite directions is  $4j-1$ , therefore, constructing that polygon is equivalent to constructing a equiangular 995-gon with sides  $4j-1$ . This construction can be proved if:
\[\sum_{i=0}^{994}[(4i+3)t^{i}]=0\]
is true. Define a complex number  $z=3+7t^{199}+11t^{398}+15t^{597}+19t^{796}$ , since  \begin{align*}
t^{5k}z
&=t^{5k}z+20kt^{5k}(0)&=3t^{5k}+7t^{5k+199}+11t^{5k+398}+15t^{5k+597}+19t^{5k+796}+20kt^{5k}(1+t^{199}+1t^{398}+1t^{597}+1t^{796})
&=(3+20k)t^{5k}+(7+20k)t^{5k+199}+(11+20k)t^{5k+398}+(15+20k)t^{5k+597}+(19+20k)t^{5k+796}
\end{align*}
Therefore:
\[z\sum_{m=1}^{199}[t^{5m}]=\sum_{i=0}^{994}[(4i+3)t^{i}]\]
The sum on the left side is the sum of 199th roots of unity multiplied by a constant  $z$  which is 0, this proves what is needed.

