This can be represented by the sum of all the coefficients of the terms that are something of the form:  $x^{0 \ (\mathrm{mod}\ p)}y^p$  of the generating function \[A(x, y) = (1+xy)(1+x^2y)\dots(1+x^{2p}y).\] Then, by the roots of unity filter, the answer is just \[\dfrac{1}{p^2}\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}A(\omega^i, \omega^j)\] where  $\omega$  is a  $p$ th primitive root of unity. Now, notice that if  $i \in [1, p-1]\cap \mathbb{Z}$ , then \[A(\omega^i, \omega^j) = (1+\omega^i \omega^j)^2(1+\omega^{2i} \omega^j)^2\dots(1+\omega^{pi} \omega^j)^2 = (1+\omega^i)^2(1+\omega^{2i})^2\dots(1+\omega^{pi})^2 = 4.\] However, if  $i = 0$ , then \[A(\omega^0, \omega^j) = (1+\omega^j)^{2p}.\] Hence, 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}A(\omega^i, \omega^j) &= 4p(p-1)+\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}(1+\omega^j)^{2p} 
&= 4p(p-1)+\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}(1+\omega^j)^{2p}.
\end{align*}
However, the sum is simply  $p$  times the sum of the coefficient of the terms whose exponent of  $x$  is divisible by  $p$  in  $(1+x)^{2p}$ . Hence, the answer is: \[\dfrac{1}{p^2}\cdot4p(p-1)+\dfrac{1}{p^2}\cdot p\left(\binom{2p}{0}+\binom{2p}{p}+\binom{2p}{2p}\right) = \dfrac{4(p-1)}{p}+\dfrac{\binom{2p}{p}+2}{p}= \boxed{\dfrac{\binom{2p}{p}+4p-2}{p}}\] as desired.  $\square$

