An  $ n \times n$  matrix whose entries come from the set  $ S \equal{} \{1, 2, \ldots , 2n \minus{} 1\}$  is called a *silver matrix* if, for each  $ i \equal{} 1, 2, \ldots , n$ , the  $ i$ -th row and the  $ i$ -th column together contain all elements of  $ S$ . Show that:

(a)  there is no silver matrix for  $ n \equal{} 1997$ ;

(b)  silver matrices exist for infinitely many values of  $ n$ .

candidates:
I claim that all odd  $n = 2k + 1$  don't work. Let the main diagonal be the diagonal from the intersection of the  $i$ -th row and column. Let a cross be the union of the  $i$ -th column and row. Notice that each entry is in at most  $2$  crosses, and thus, each element shows up at least  $k + 1$  times. However, since  $(4k + 1)(k + 1) > (2k + 1)^2,$  this is impossible.

I claim that all numbers that can be expressed as  $2^n$  are valid. I will prove this with induction. $n = 1$  is valid
\begin{center}
     $
    \begin{bmatrix}
        1 & 2 
        3 & 1
    \end{bmatrix}
    $ \end{center} 
Now, let  $n = k$  be true, with the following construction:
\begin{center}
     $\begin{bmatrix}
        1 & a_{2, 1} & \cdots & a_{2^{k}, 1} 
        a_{1, 2} & 1 & & \vdots
        \vdots & & \ddots  
        a_{1, 2^k} & \cdots & & 1
    \end{bmatrix}$ \end{center} 
Then, it follows that  $n = k+1$  is true because of the following construction:
\begin{center}
     $\begin{bmatrix}
        1 & a_{2, 1} & \cdots & a_{2^{k}, 1} & 1 + 2^k & a_{2, 1} + 2^k & \cdots & a_{2^{k}, 1} + 2^k 
        a_{1, 2} & \ddots & & \vdots & a_{1, 2} + 2^k & \ddots & & \vdots 
        \vdots & & & & \vdots  
        a_{1, 2^k} & \cdots & & 1 & a_{1, 2^k} + 2^k & \cdots & & 1 + 2^k 

        2^k & a_{2, 1} + 2^k & \cdots & a_{2^{k}, 1} + 2^k & 1 & a_{2, 1} & \cdots & a_{2^{k}, 1}
        a_{1, 2} + 2^k & 2^k & & \vdots & a_{1, 2} & \ddots & & \vdots 
        \vdots & & \ddots & & \vdots  
        a_{1, 2^k} + 2^k & \cdots & & 2^k & a_{1, 2^k} & \cdots & & 1 
    \end{bmatrix}$ \end{center} 
It is easy to check that this works. 

Hence, because all  $2^n$ -sized matrices work, there are an infinite number of \textit{silver} matrices.

