*Same as all of the other bashy Schur solutions*  :-D **Solution**. Begin with the standard substitution  $$ a = \frac{x}{y}, \quad b = \frac{y}{z}, \quad c = \frac{z}{x}. $$  then for any values of  $x$ ,  $y$ ,  $z \in \mathbb{R}^+$ , we have  $abc = 1$ . Rewriting our inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
	\left( a - 1 + \frac 1b \right) \left( b - 1 + \frac 1c \right) \left( c - 1 + \frac 1a \right) &\leq 1 
	\iff \left(\frac{x}{y} - 1 + \frac{z}{y}\right)\left(\frac{y}{z} - 1 + \frac{x}{z}\right)\left(\frac{z}{x} - 1 + \frac{y}{x}\right) & \leq 1 
	\iff \left(\frac{x - y + z}{y}\right) \left(\frac{x + y - z}{z}\right)  \left(\frac{-x + y + z}{z}\right) & \leq 1 
	\iff (x - y + z)(x + y - z)(-x + y + z)& \leq xyz.
\end{align*}
This is a symmetric, homogeneous inequality, so a good thing to try (unless you see a different, cleaner way to do, which there surely is) is just expanding everything out. Thus it suffices to show
\begin{align*}
	(x - y + z)(x + y - z)(-x + y + z)& \leq xyz 
	\iff 0 \leq xyz - (x - y + z)(x + y - z)(-x + y + z) 
	\iff 0 \leq x^3 + y^3 + z^3 - x^2 y - xy^2 - x^2 z - xz^2 - yz^2 - y^2 z + 3xyz 
	\iff x^2y + x^2 z + xy^2 + xz^2 + y^2 z + yz^2 \leq x^3 + y^3 + z^3 + 3xyz.
\end{align*}
When you see seomthing like this you should have probably four things in mind: AM-GM, Muirhead, Schur and sums of squares. In this case, a quick glance will show you that Muirhead isn't quite strong enough, but Schur seems to work fine. Rewriting in cyclic sum notation to make the inequality clearer, we wish to show
\begin{align*}
	\sum_{\text{cyc}} x^3  + \sum_{\text{cyc}} xyz &\geq \sum_{\text{cyc}} x^2y + \sum_{\text{cyc}}x^2z 
	\iff \sum_{\text{cyc}} x(x^2 + yz) &\geq \sum_{\text{cyc}} x^2(y + z),
\end{align*}
which is exactly the statement of Schur's inequality! That means this is true, and we are done.

