Since  $AB$  is tangent to circles  $G_1$  and  $G_2,$  we know that  $\angle BAM = \angle CAM.$  Also,  $\angle CAM = \angle ANM$  because they inscribe the same arc. However,  $\angle BAM = \angle CMA$  because  $AB\parallel CD.$  Since  $\angle CMA = \angle CNA,$   $\overline{AN}$  bisects  $\angle CNM.$  Thus,  $\triangle CAM$  is isosceles. With the same logic,  $\triangle MBD$  is isosceles too.

Also,  $\angle EAB = \angle ECD$  and  $\angle EBA = \angle EDC.$  Since  $\angle EAB = \angle AMC = \angle BAM$  and  $\angle EBA = \angle BMD = \angle ABM,$  we know that  $\triangle EAB \cong \triangle MAB.$  Thus,  $\overline{EA} = \overline{AM}$  and  $\overline{EB} = \overline{BM}.$  On a side note, this proves that  $CD = 2\cdot AB.$ Note that  $\angle AEB = 180^{\circ} - (\angle EAB + \angle EBA).$  Also note that  $\angle EBA = \angle BNE$  and  $\angle EAB = \angle ANE.$  Thus,  $\angle AEB = 180^{\circ} - \angle ANB.$  This shows that quadrilateral  $EANB$  is cyclic.

Hence,  $\angle ABN = \angle AEN.$  Since  $\angle ABN = \angle BQD = \angle CQN,$  this means that  $\angle ABN = \angle CQN.$  Thus, quadrilateral  $ECNQ$  is cyclic. With similar logic, quadrilateral  $EPND$  is also cyclic.

It follows that  $\angle ECN = \angle EQB$  because  $ECNQ$  is cyclic. Similarly,  $\angle EDN = \angle APE$  because  $EPND$  is cyclic.   $\angle ECN =  \angle BAN = \angle APC$  since  $AB$  is tangent to  $G_1.$  Similarly,  $\angle EDN = \angle ABN = \angle BQD$  because  $AB$  is tangent to  $G_2.$  Thus,  $\angle EPC = \angle EQD,$  which means  $\angle EPQ = \angle EQP.$  Therefore,  $EP = EQ$  as desired. $\quad\Box$

