We all know that the number  $2000$  is of no use and the actual problem is to show that there exist a positive integer  $n$ , where it has exactly  $d$  prime divisors and  $n \mid 2^n+1$  for any value  $d$ .
We prove this using induction. Note that for a prime  $p$  that divides  $n$ , it cannot be equal to  $2$  as  $2^n+1$  is always odd.

Before using induction note thhe following.
Now take  $p$  to be the smallest prime factor of  $n$ . Note that,
\begin{align*}
2^n &\equiv -1 \pmod{p} 
2^{2n} &\equiv 1 \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
From Fermat's little theorem we have that,   $$ 2^{p-1} \equiv 1\pmod{p} $$  Combining the above two we have that,  $$  2^{\gcd(2n,p-1)} \equiv 1 \pmod {p} $$  Note that as we defined  $p$  to be the smallest prime factor of  $n$ , we can't have  $p-1 \mid n$ . Hence,  $$ \gcd(2n,p-1) = \gcd(2,p-1) = 2 $$  \begin{align*}
\implies 2^2 &\equiv 1 \pmod{p} 
\implies 3 &\equiv 0 \pmod{p} 

\implies p &= 3
\end{align*}

<span style="color:#3D85C6">**<u>Base case :</u>**</span>
Note that   $$ 3 \mid 2^3 + 1 $$  <span style="color:#45818E">**<u>Inductive step :</u>**</span>

Now let  $n_k = 3^l \cdot p_1p_2 \dots p_k$ , where  $p_i$  ;  $i={1,2, \dots k}$  are prime factors of  $n$  . Assume that   $$ n _k \mid 2^{n_k} + 1 $$  We'll prove that  $n_{k+1} \mid 2^{n_{k+1}} + 1$ . Note that  $n_{k+1} = 3n_k \cdot p_{k+1}$ , where  $p_{k+1}$  is a prime not equal to { $p_1,p_2, \dots p_k$ }.
From Zsignmondy's theorem we have that there exist a prime  $p_{k+1}$  such that,   $$ p_{k+1} \mid 3^{n_{k+1}} +1 $$  and  $p_{k+1} \nmid 3^{n_k} +1$ . Which implies that  $p_{k+1}$  is not equal to { $p_1,p_2, \dots p_k$ }. Now it suffices to prove that  $3n_k \mid 2^{n_{k+1}} + 1 $ .
From Lifting the Exponent Lemma we have that,   $$   v_3(3n_{k}) = k+1 < k+2 = (k+1) + 1  = v_3(n_{k+1})+v_3(2+1) = v_3(2^{n_{k+1}}) = $$  Hence, it suffices to prove that  $p_{i} \mid 2^{n_{k+1}}+1$  where  $i={1,2, \dots,k}$ . Note that we have 
\begin{align*}
2^{n_k} &\equiv -1 \pmod{p_i} 
(2^{n_k})^{3\cdot p_{k+1}} &\equiv -1 \pmod{p_i} 
\implies 2^{n_{k+1}} &\equiv -1 \pmod{p_i} 

\implies p_{i} &\mid 2^{n_{k+1}}+1
\end{align*}


Therefore from the Principle of Mathematical Induction we prove our claim

