I was fooling around with problems related to Simsons Line and ended up with this one :P 
<blockquote>Let  $ABCD$  be a cyclic quadrilateral. Let  $P$ ,  $Q$ ,  $R$  be the feet of the perpendiculars from  $D$  to the lines  $BC$ ,  $CA$ ,  $AB$ , respectively. Show that  $PQ=QR$  if and only if the bisectors of  $\angle ABC$  and  $\angle ADC$  are concurrent with  $AC$ .</blockquote> $  $ We shall prove the only if part cause the other one follows conversely.
Denote by  $X$  the concurrency point of the angle bisectors of  $\angle ADC$  and  $\angle ABC$  with AC. WLOG, assume  $\angle A > \angle C$ . $  $ We proceed by noting that the points  $R,P$  and  $Q$  are collinear as they determine the  $D-$  Simsons line of  $\triangle ABC$ . Now the rest is just a trigonometric maneuver. Let  $\alpha_{1} = \angle BAC ; \alpha_{2} = \angle CAD ; \gamma_{1} = \angle ACB ; \gamma_{2} = \angle ACD$ . $  $ As  $\angle ARD = \angle AQD = 90^{\circ}$ , we have that quadrilateral  $ARDQ$  is cyclic. Analogously quadrilateral  $CPQD$  is also cyclic. Therefore we have:
\begin{align*}
\angle RDQ  & = \angle RDA + \angle ADQ 
                      & = (90^{\circ}-\angle RAD) + (90^{\circ} - \angle CAD)
                      & = 180^{\circ} - \angle C - \alpha_{2}
                      & = \alpha_{1}
\end{align*}    
Also,  $\angle QRD = \angle CAD = \alpha_{2}$ . Thus, by sine rule in  $\triangle RDQ$  we get that :  $$  QR = DQ \cdot \left(\frac{\sin {\alpha_{1}}}{\sin {\alpha_{2}}}\right) \dots (\star) $$  Similarly by sine rule in  $\triangle PDQ$ :  $$  PQ = DQ \cdot \left(\frac{\sin {\gamma_{1}}}{\sin {\gamma_{2}}}\right) \dots (\star \star) $$  Comparing  $(\star)$  and  $(\star \star)$  we see that:  $$  \frac{PQ}{QR} = \frac{\sin {\alpha_{1}}}{\sin {\gamma_{1}}} \cdot \frac{\sin {\gamma_{2}}}{\sin {\alpha_{2}}} $$  Since  $BX$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle ABC \implies \frac{\sin {\alpha_{1}}}{\sin {\alpha_{2}}} = \frac{AB}{BC} = \frac{AX}{CX}$ . Similarly, since  $DX$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle ADC \implies  \frac{\sin{\alpha_{2}}}{\sin{\gamma_{2}}} = \frac{AD}{DC} = \frac{AX}{CX}$ . $  $ Hence,   $$  \frac{PQ}{QR} = \frac{\sin {\alpha_{1}}}{\sin {\gamma_{1}}} \cdot \frac{\sin {\gamma_{2}}}{\sin {\alpha_{2}}} = \frac{AX}{CX} \cdot \frac{CX}{AX} = 1 \iff \boxed{PQ = QR} $$

candidates:
In fact, \(ABCD\) need not be cyclic.

Observe that \(Q\), \(R\) lie on the circle with diameter \(AC\) and \(Q\), \(P\) lie on the circle with diameter \(DC\). By the extended law of sines, \(QR=AD\sin\angle RAQ=AD\sin\angle BAC\) and \(QP=CD\sin\angle PCQ=CD\sin\angle BCA\). Hence \[\frac{QR}{QP}=\frac{AD}{CD}\cdot\frac{\sin\angle BAC}{\sin\angle BCA}=\frac{AD}{CD}\cdot\frac{BC}{BA}.\]

