dot("\(A\)",A,W);     dot("\(B\)",B,B);     dot("\(C\)",C,E);     dot("\(D\)",D,S);     dot("\(P\)",P,NE);     dot("\(Q\)",Q,N);     dot("\(R\)",R,W); [/asy]

Then \(QR=QP\) if and only if \(AD/CD=BA/BC\), which implies the desired concurrence by angle bisector theorem.

Suppose  $PR = RQ$ . Note that  $DQCP$  and  $DARQ$  are cyclic with diameters  $DC$  and  $DA$  respectively. By the Law of Sines, we have
\begin{align*}
	\frac{PQ}{\sin C} &= DC 
	\frac{RQ}{\sin A} &= AD.
\end{align*}
Hence,
\begin{align*}
	\frac{\sin A}{\sin C} &= \frac{DC}{AD} 
	\frac{BC}{AB} &= \frac{DC}{AD},
\end{align*}
which is enough by Angle Bisector Theorem. The other direction is identical.

Let  $M$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{AC}$ .  Note that  $\triangle DPR\sim\triangle DAC$  (angle chasing with the cyclic quads you get from the projections!), so
\begin{align*}
PQ=QR&\text{ if and only if }\angle QDR = \angle MDC
             &\text{ if and only if }\angle ADB = \angle MDC
             &\text{ if and only if }BD\text{ is a symmedian of }\triangle ADC
             &\text{ if and only if }ABCD\text{ is harmonic }
             &\text{ if and only if the bisectors concur on  $\overline{AC}$ .}
\end{align*}

Some projective overkill -- some points in the solution omitted in the diagram, because they are too far away from the central  $ABCD$ . 

