Notice that the bisector concurrency condition is equivalent to  $\frac{AB}{BC} = \frac{AD}{DC}$  by the angle bisector theorem, which implies  $ABCD$  is harmonic, and that  $PQR$  is just the Simson line of  $ABC$  with respect to  $D$  and thus collinear. Next, denote by  $P_\infty$  the point at  infinity along  $PQR$ ,  $X = \overline{AC} \cap \overline{BP_\infty}$  and  $B' \neq B = \overline{BP_\infty} \cap (ABCD)$ . By [a well-known lemma,](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c618937h1627338p10205043)  $D, Q, B'$  are collinear.

Now taking perspectivity at  $B$  and  $B'$ , respectively,   $$ -1 = (P, R; Q, P_\infty) \stackrel B= (C, A; Q, X) \stackrel {B'}=(C, A; D, B) = (A, C; B, D), $$   which is equivalent to  $ABCD$  being harmonic, as desired.  $\square$

WLOG, let  $D$  be on arc  $BC$  that doesn't contain  $A$ . By Simson's,  $P-Q-R$  are collinear.

By law of sine in  $\triangle DQR, \triangle DQP$ , we have
\begin{align}
\triangle DQR: \frac{\sin DRQ}{\sin RDQ}&=\frac{DQ}{RQ} 
\triangle DQP: \frac{\sin DPQ}{\sin QDP}&=\frac{DQ}{QP}
\end{align} $\frac{(1)}{(2)}: \frac{\sin DRQ}{\sin RDQ}\cdot \frac{\sin QDP}{\sin DPQ}=1$  since  $PQ=QR$ .

By simple angle chasing, we have


- $\angle DRQ = \angle DAC$
- $\angle RDQ = \angle CAB$
- $\angle QDP = \angle ACB$
- $\angle DPQ = \angle DCA$

Thus,  $\frac{\sin DAC}{\sin CAB}\cdot\frac{\sin ACB}{\sin DCA}=1$ . Let  $R$  be the radius of  $(ABCD).$ \begin{align*}
\frac{\frac{2R}{\sin CAB}}{\frac{2R}{\sin DAC}}\cdot\frac{\frac{2R}{\sin DCA}}{\frac{2R}{\sin ACB}}=1
&\implies\frac{CB}{DC}\cdot\frac{DA}{AB}=1 
&\implies \frac{AB}{BC}=\frac{AD}{DC}
\end{align*}
Let the angle bisector of  $\angle ABC, \angle ADC$  meet  $AC$  at  $X,Y$ , respectively.   $$ \frac{AB}{BC}=\frac{AD}{DC}\implies \frac{AX}{XC}=\frac{AY}{YC}\implies X\equiv Y $$

... or if we want to be stupid we just use the same complex number formula three times and get the answer.

WLOG let  $(ABCD)$  be the unit circle. Then we compute
\begin{align*}
p&=\frac{1}{2}\left(b+c+d-\frac{bc}{d}\right),
q&=\frac{1}{2}\left(a+c+d-\frac{ac}{d}\right),
r&=\frac{1}{2}\left(a+b+d-\frac{ab}{d}\right).
\end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*}
p-q&=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{c}{d}-1\right)(a-b),
r-q&=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{a}{d}-1\right)(c-b).
\end{align*}
Thus  $$ PQ=QR \iff |p-q|=|r-q| \iff \frac{|c-d|}{|a-d|}=\frac{|c-b|}{|a-b|} \iff \frac{CD}{AD}=\frac{CB}{AB}, $$  which clearly is equivalent to the concurrence on the angle bisectors.  $\blacksquare$

Let's set  $x_i=a_{i-1}+\dots+a_1+x_1$ , where the  $a_j$ 's are non-negative. Then we have \begin{align*} \frac 23 (n^2-1) \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n (x_i-x_j)^2 & = \frac 43 (n^2-1) \sum_{i=2}^n \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (x_i-x_j)^2  & = \frac 43 (n^2-1) \sum_{i=2}^n \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (a_{i-1}+a_{i-2}+\dots+a_j)^2  & = \frac 43 (n^2-1) \left [ \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} k(n-k) a_k^2+2\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}\sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1} i(n-j) a_i a_j \right ].\end{align*}
And similarly, \begin{align*} \left (\sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n |x_i-x_j|\right)^2 & = 4 \left (\sum_{i=2}^n \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (x_i-x_j)\right)^2  & =  4 \left (\sum_{i=2}^n \sum_{j=1}^{i-1} (a_{i-1}+a_{i-2}+\dots+a_j)\right)^2  & = 4\left [ \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} k^2(n-k)^2 a_k^2+2\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}\sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1} ij(n-i)(n-j) a_i a_j \right].\end{align*}
<details><summary>original fakesolve; kept for amusement ;)</summary>So we need to prove that,   $$ \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \left [ k(n-k)(n^2-1)-3k^2(n-k)^2\right ]a_k^2 \geqslant \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}\sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1} \left [ 3ij(n-i)(n-j)-i(n^2-1)(n-j)\right ] 2a_ia_j \qquad (1) $$  For convenience, let us define  $f(i,j)=3ij(n-i)(n-j)-i(n^2-1)(n-j)$ . Note that  $f(i,j)>0$  when  $j>i$  (<span style="color:#f00">unfortunately, this isn't true. I'll try to fix soon. Hopefully this doesn't turn out to be a fakesolve</span>). So we have   $$ \sum_{i=1}^{n-2} \sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1} f(i,j) \cdot 2a_i a_j \leqslant \sum_{i=1}^{n-2} \sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1} f(i,j)(a_i^2+a_j^2) = \sum_{i=1}^{n-2} \left (\sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1} f(i,j)\right) a_i^2 +\sum_{k=2}^{n-1} \left (\sum_{i=1}^{k-1} f(i,k)\right ) a_k^2 \qquad (2) $$   Now it's not very hard to calculate the sums inside parenthesis; we'll find  $(2)$  is exactly equal to the LHS of  $(1)$ . Also, equality holds iff  $2a_i a_j=a_i^2+a_j^2$  for all  $i,j$ , i.e. when  $a_1=a_2=\dots=a_{n-1}$ . This is the same as  $x_1,x_2,\dots,x_n$  being arithmetic. So we're done.</details>

So we need to prove that   $$ F(a_1,a_2,\dots,a_{n-1}):=\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \left [ k(n-k)(n^2-1)-3k^2(n-k)^2\right ]a_k^2 -\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}\sum_{j=i+1}^{n-1} \left [ 3ij(n-i)(n-j)-i(n^2-1)(n-j)\right ] 2a_ia_j\geqslant 0 \qquad (1) $$   The inequality is homogeneous, so we can assume wlog that   $$ a_1+\dots+a_{n-1}=1\qquad (2) $$   So that the domain of  $F$  becomes  $[0,1]^{n-1}$  which is compact. So  $F$  attains a global minimum. 

First, suppose  $\{a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}\}\subset \{0,1\}$ . If one of the  $a_i$ 's is  $1$  and the rest are  $0$ , then  $F>0$  because  $k(n-k)(n^2-1)-3k^2(n-k)^2>0$  is true when  $1\leq k<n$ . And when all are  $0$  then obviously  $F=0$ . Now suppose  $a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}\in (0,1)$  so that we can proceed with Lagrange Multipliers. Taking the gradients of  $F$  and the constraint function  $a_1+\dots+a_{n-1}$  we get the equations \begin{align*} & 2(k(n-k)(n^2-1)-3k^2(n-k)^2)a_k-2\sum_{i=1}^{k-1} (3ik(n-i)(n-k)-i(n^2-1)(n-k))a_i  & \qquad -2\sum_{j=k+1}^{n-1} (3kj(n-k)(n-j)-k(n^2-1)(n-j))a_j=\lambda, \quad (k=1,2,\dots, n-1) \qquad (3)\end{align*} (Take the empty sum to be  $0$ ). Notice that we have  $n$  variables  $\lambda,a_1,\dots,a_{n-1}$  and  $n$  linear independent (this is not very hard to see?) equations  $(2)$  and  $(3)$ . So if there is a solution, it must be unique. We claim the solution is   $$ a_1=a_2=\dots=a_{n-1}=\frac{1}{n-1}, \quad \lambda=0. $$   Indeed this solution satisfies  $(2)$  and we can show without much effort that   $$ \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} (3ik(n-i)(n-k)-i(n^2-1)(n-k))+\sum_{j=k+1}^{n-1} (3kj(n-k)(n-j)-k(n^2-1)(n-j))=k(n-k)(n^2-1)-3k^2(n-k)^2 \qquad (4) $$   so it also satisfies  $(3)$ . Finally, showing  $F\left (\frac{1}{n-1},\frac{1}{n-1},\dots,\frac{1}{n-1}\right)=0$  is pretty much the same as proving  $(4)$ , so we'll omit this too. 

Thus the global minimum of  $F$  is  $0$ . This proves  $(1)$  and so the original inequality as well. Also the equality holds iff  $a_1=\dots=a_{n-1}$ , as seen above. This implies that the equality case of the original inequality is when  $x_1, \dots, x_n$  is arithmetic. This finishes the problem.

