Define a "hook" to be a figure made up of six unit squares as shown below in the picture, or any of the figures obtained by applying rotations and reflections to this figure. 

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

draw((0,0)--(1,0));
draw((0,1)--(1,1));
draw((2,1)--(3,1));
draw((0,2)--(3,2));
draw((0,3)--(3,3));
draw((0,0)--(0,3));
draw((1,0)--(1,3));
draw((2,1)--(2,3));
draw((3,1)--(3,3));
[/asy]
Determine all  $ m\times n$  rectangles that can  be covered without gaps and without overlaps with hooks such that 

- the rectangle is covered without gaps and without overlaps
- no part of a hook covers area outside the rectangle.

candidates:
Clearly, if there exists a hook, then the square in the middle must be part of another hook. The only valid options are the following two:
[center]
[asy]
size(6cm,0);
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,3)--(2,3)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(0,2)--(0,0));
draw((1,2)--(1,4)--(4,4)--(4,2)--(3,2));
draw((6,0)--(6,3)--(10,3)--(10,0)--(6,0));
draw((7,0)--(7,2)--(8,2)--(8,1)--(9,1)--(9,3));
[/asy]
[/center]
In particular, there must be an even number of hooks, so the number of grid is a multiple of  $12$ . From now on, we will ban rotations and instead add two more figures that we can tile with. Reflections are still allowed. Denote the first one by  $A$  and its ninety degree rotation by  $B$ . Denote the second one by  $C$  and its ninety degree rotation by  $D$ .

If we color every other row red, each type of figure will cover six red squares, except figure  $B$ , which will cover four or eight. Similarly, if we color every other column red, each type of figure will cover six red squres, except figure  $D$ , which will cover four or eight. If we group two squares horizontally and apply chessboard to those groups, each type of figure will cover six red squares, except figure  $C$ , which will cover four or eight. If we do the same vertically we get similar result for  $A$ . 

Thus, if both  $m$  and  $n$  are even, then we have proved that an even number of each figure is needed, so  $24\mid mn$ . Then, one of them must be divisible by  $4$ . If not both  $m$  and  $n$  are even,  $12\mid mn$  forces that anyway. WLOG, assume  $4\mid m$ . Then, either we have  $12\mid m$  or  $4\mid m$ ,  $3\mid n$ . The second one is obviously possible and the first is possible for any  $n=3a+4b$  for some nonnegative integers  $a,b$ . That is,  $n$  can equal anything that isn't  $1$ ,  $2$ , or  $5$  and it's each to check that those don't work.

We claim that the answer is all rectangles  $m \times n$  where  $3 \mid m$  or  $3 \mid n$  (say  $3\mid m$ ) and if  $12 \nmid m$  then  $4 \mid n$  and if  $12 \mid m $  then  $n>2$  but  $n \ne 5$ .

<span style="color:#00f">**Showing all  $m \times n$  rectangles of the given dimensions can be tiled.**</span>Note that a  $3 \times 4$  grid can be tiled using two hooks as follows.
[asy]
unitsize(0.75cm);

for (int i=0; i<=4; ++i) {
  if((i!=0)&&(i!=4)) {
	for (int j=0; j<=3; ++j) {
      draw( (0,j)--(4,j), grey );
draw( (i,0)--(i,3), grey );
    }
  }
}

draw((0,0)--(0,3)--(4,3)--(4,0)--cycle, black+1);

filldraw( (0.1,0.1)--(2.9,0.1)--(2.9,1.9)--(2.1,1.9)--(2.1,0.9)--(0.9,0.9)--(0.9,2.9)--(0.1,2.9)--cycle, opacity(0.2)+palered, red+1 );

filldraw( (3.9,2.9)--(3.9,0.1)--(3.1,0.1)--(3.1,2.1)--(1.9,2.1)--(1.9,1.1)--(1.1,1.1)--(1.1,2.9)--cycle, opacity(0.2)+lightgreen, blue+1 );
[/asy]
If  $3 \mid m$  and  $4\mid n$  clearly we can tile the  $m \times n$  rectangle by  $3 \times 4$  subrectangles, and when  $12 \mid m$  and  $n>2$  but  $n \ne 5$  we can first tile a  $12 \times n$  grid by considering  $n=3a+4b$  (which has solutions for all  $n>5$ ) and then tile the  $m \times n$  rectangle using these  $12 \times n$  subrectangles.

<span style="color:#00f">**Showing all other rectangles cannot be tiled.**</span>The following is the key observation. 

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :**Any valid tiling uses an even number of hooks.</span>

*Proof :*The idea is that hooks come in pairs. Let a hook be called the *buddy* of another hook, if it covers the 'center square' of the hook. No hook can have two buddies, and clearly buddiness is a commutative property. Note that if hook  $\mathcal{H}_1$  is the buddy of hook  $\mathcal{H}_2$  the center square of hook  $\mathcal{H}_2$  must be covered by one of the two 'end' squares of  $\mathcal{H}_1$  since any other square in a hook has two orthogonal neighbours both of which belong to the same hook, while the center square of a hook has only one uncovered neighbour.

Hence, it is clear that hooks always come in pairs which form compound tiles which are either  $3 \times 4$  rectangles (referred to as *plates*) or  $4 \times 4$  rectangles with two similarly oriented dominoes deleted from the opposite corners (referred to as *dogs*). 

Now, since plates and dogs are all of area  $12$ , the area of the rectangle must be a multiple of  $12$ . Hence, if  $3 \mid m$  and  $2 \nmid m$  it follows that  $4 \mid n$  as desired, and if  $\nu_2(m)=1$  it follows that  $\nu_2(n)\ge 1$ . 

Now, we deal with the case where  $6 \mid m$  but  $12 \nmid m$  and  $\nu_2(n)=1$ .

Color the rectangle black and white where every other square of every other row is colored black, with the top-left corner square colored black. It is easy to check that both dogs and plates cover an even number of black squares (2 or 4 and 4 respectively). However, in the above case we have exactly  $\frac{mn}{4}$  black squares which is odd, a clear contradiction.

solution_md:
One can show the bound of  $(n+\sqrt{10} - 3)^2$  by simple expanding also:
[rule]
Suppose  $t_1 \ge t_2 + t_3$ . Few Observations:


- If  $x \ge t_2 + t_3$  then  $f(x) = \frac{x}{t_2} + \frac{t_2}{x} + \frac{x}{t_3} + \frac{t_3}{x} \ge 5$ . <details><summary>Proof</summary>Using AM-GM we obtain
\begin{align*}
f(x) &\ge \frac{4x}{t_2 + t_3} + \frac{t_2 + t_3}{x} 
= \frac{x}{t_2 + t_3} + \frac{x}{t_2 + t_3} + \frac{x}{t_2 + t_3} + \frac{x}{t_2 + t_3} + \frac{t_2 + t_3}{x} 
\ge 5 \cdot \sqrt[5]{\frac{x^3}{(t_2 + t_3)^2}}
&\ge 5
\end{align*}
Note that  $x_2 \ge t_2 + t_3$  is only used in the last step. One could also finish by looking at  $\frac{4x}{t_2 + t_3} + \frac{t_2 + t_3}{x}$  as a function in  $x$  and differentiating to get it is increasing in  $[t_2 + t_3,\infty)$ .</details>
- For any  $y > 0$  we have  $g(y) \ge \frac{t_1 + t_2 + t_3}{y} + \frac{y}{t_1} + \frac{y}{t_2} + \frac{y}{t_3} \ge 2 \sqrt{10}$ . <details><summary>Proof</summary>If  $y \ge t_2 + t_3$ , then one could use  $f(y) \ge 5$  along with  $\frac{t_1}{y} + \frac{y}{t_1} \ge 2$  to get  $g(y) \ge 7$ . Otherwise, if  $y \le t_2 + t_3$ , then we may assume  $t_1 = t_2 + t_3$  as changing  $t_1 \to t_2 + t_3$  would decrease value of  $y$ . Then,   $$ f(y) \ge \frac{2t_1}{y} + \frac{y}{t_1} + \frac{4y}{t_2 + t_3} = \frac{2t_1}{y} + \frac{5y}{t_1} \ge 2 \sqrt{10} $$</details>

Now expanding gives 
\begin{align*}
\text{RHS} &= n + \sum_{4 \le i < j \le n} \left( \frac{t_i}{t_j} + \frac{t_j}{t_i} \right) + \sum_{i \ne 1,2,3} g(t_i) + \sum_{1 \le i < j \le 3} \left( \frac{t_i}{t_j} + \frac{t_j}{t_i} \right) \ge n + (n-3)(n-4) + (n-3)(2 \sqrt{10}) + 7 
&= \boxed{n^2 + 1 + (n-3)(2 \sqrt{10} - 6) = (n + \sqrt{10} - 3)^2}
\end{align*}
For  $t_1=10,t_2=t_3=5,t_4 = \cdots = t_n = 2 \sqrt{10}$ , equality indeed holds. Done!  $\blacksquare$

candidates:
Brute force:

We will prove that,  $t_1+t_2>t_3$ ; and this will establish the proof, through symmetry. First, begin by, opening the parantheses, on the right hand side:  $$ n^2 + 1 > n + \sum_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\frac{t_i}{t_j}+\frac{t_j}{t_i} \iff 1>n+\sum_{1\leq i<j\leq n}\frac{t_i}{t_j}+\frac{t_j}{t_i}-2. $$  Now, we will isolate the terms, corresponding to the following pairs:  $(i,j)=(1,3)$ ; and,  $(i,j)=(2,3)$ . We have,  $$ 1>\underbrace{\frac{t_1}{t_3}+\frac{t_2}{t_3}+\frac{t_3}{t_2}+\frac{t_3}{t_1}-4}_{\triangleq S'} +\underbrace{\sum_{\substack{1\leq i<j\leq n  (i,j)\neq (1,3),(2,3)}}\frac{t_i}{t_j}+\frac{t_j}{t_i}-2}_{\triangleq S}. $$  Clearly,  $S\geq 0$ . We will now, establish that, if,  $t_3>t_1+t_2$ ; then,  $S'\geq 1$ ; hence, giving us a contradiction. To that end, let,  $t_3=t_1+t_2+\epsilon$ , with,  $\epsilon\geq 0$ . We can now, first, substitute for  $t_3$ ; and then, bound  $S'$  from below, as follows:
\newpage
\begin{align*}
S'&=\frac{t_1+t_2}{t_1+t_2+\epsilon} + \frac{t_1}{t_2}+1+\frac{\epsilon}{t_2}+1+\frac{t_2}{t_1}+\frac{\epsilon}{t_1}-4
&=\frac{t_1+t_2}{t_1+t_2+\epsilon}+\frac{\epsilon}{t_1}+\frac{\epsilon}{t_2}+\underbrace{\frac{t_1}{t_2}+\frac{t_2}{t_1}-2}_{\geq 0}
&\geq \frac{t_1+t_2}{t_1+t_2+\epsilon}+\frac{\epsilon}{t_1}+\frac{\epsilon}{t_2}
&=\frac{t_1+t_2}{t_1+t_2+\epsilon}+\frac{\epsilon(t_1+t_2)}{t_1t_2}
&=\frac{t_1t_2(t_1+t_2)+\epsilon(t_1+t_2)(t_1+t_2+\epsilon)}{t_1t_2(t_1+t_2+\epsilon)}
&=\frac{t_1t_2(t_1+t_2+\epsilon)+\Delta}{t_1t_2(t_1+t_2+\epsilon)}>1,
\end{align*}
where,  $\Delta=\epsilon(t_1^2+t_1t_2+t_2^2)+\epsilon^2(t_1+t_2)$ ; giving us,  $S'\geq 1$ .

No induction or Cauchy

WLOG let  $t_1 \leq t_2 \leq \cdots \leq t_n$ . It suffices to show that  $t_1+t_2>t_n$ . Suppose otherwise; I will show that the RHS is at least  $n^2+1$ .
First note that by Jensen's if  $t_3, \ldots, t_{n-1}$  are not all equal, we can set them to their average, which preserves the size ordering and only decreases the RHS. Also by Jensen's, if  $t_1 \neq t_2$ , we can set them to their average, which doesn't change the truth of  $t_1+t_2>t_n$  and also only decreases the RHS. Finally, we can scale such that  $t_3=\cdots=t_{n-1}=1$ , whence we let  $t_1=t_2=a$  and  $t_n=b$ . Then,  $2a \leq b \implies \tfrac{b}{a} \geq 2$ , and
\begin{align*}
( t_1 + t_2 + \cdots + t_n) \left( \frac{1}{t_1} + \frac{1}{t_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{t_n} \right)&=((n-3)+2a+b)\left((n-3)+\frac{2}{a}+\frac{1}{b}\right)
&=(n-3)^2+\left(2a+\frac{2}{a}+b+\frac{1}{b}\right)(n-3)+4+1+\frac{2a}{b}+\frac{2b}{a}
&\geq (n-3)^2+6(n-3)+5+\frac{2a}{b}+\frac{2b}{a}
&=n^2-4+\frac{2b}{a}+\frac{2a}{b}.
\end{align*}
By taking the derivative we can verify that the function  $f(x)=x+\tfrac{1}{x}$  is increasing on  $[2,\infty)$ , so  $$ 2f\left(\frac{b}{a}\right)\geq 2f(2)=5, $$  hence  $n^2-4+\frac{2b}{a}+\frac{2a}{b}>n^2+1$  which is the desired conclusion.  $\blacksquare$ **Remark:** We can actually smooth a bit more to reduce to the case where  $2a=b$  I think. Then  $2a+\tfrac{2}{a}+b+\tfrac{1}{b}=4a+\tfrac{5/2}{a} \geq 2\sqrt{10}$  by AM-GM, which improves the bound by  $(2\sqrt{10}-6)(n-3) \approx (0.32455532)(n-3)$ .

Assume that  $t_1\le t_2\le\dots \le t_n$ . It suffices to show that  $t_1+t_2>t_n$ . Assume otherwise; then  $t_1+t_2\le t_n$ . I claim that
\[s=\frac{t_1}{t_2}+\frac{t_2}{t_1}+\frac{t_2}{t_n}+\frac{t_n}{t_2}+\frac{t_n}{t_1}+\frac{t_1}{t_n}\ge 7.\]
Notice that as  $t_n$  increases, the value of  $s$  also increases, since the values of  $\frac{t_2}{t_n}+\frac{t_n}{t_1}$  and  $\frac{t_n}{t_1}+\frac{t_1}{t_n}$  will increase. Therefore, it suffices to show that when  $t_n=t_1+t_2$ , the claim holds true. But now, we obtain:
\begin{align*}
s &= \frac{t_2+t_n}{t_1}+\frac{t_1+t_n}{t_2}+\frac{t_1+t_2}{t_n} 
&= 3+2\left(\frac{t_1}{t_2}+\frac{t_2}{t_1}\right) 
&\ge 7,
\end{align*}
so the claim is proven.
Now, we have that
\begin{align*}
(t_1+t_2+\dots+t_n)\left(\frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\dots+\frac{1}{t_n}\right)&= n+\sum_{1\le i<j\le n}\left(\frac{t_i}{t_j}+\frac{t_j}{t_i}\right) 
&\ge n+s+2\left(\frac{n^2-n}{2}-3\right) 
&\ge n^2+1,
\end{align*}
a contradiction. We are done.  $\blacksquare$

I don't remember my solution being this messy :P

We proceed via induction.**Base case:** set  $n = 3$ . Suppose without loss of generality that  $t_1 + t_2 \leq t_3$ . Now we let \[a = \frac{t_1}{t_3}, b = \frac{t_2}{t_3}\] for convenience so that  $a + b \leq 1$ . We have (where  $s = a + b \leq 1$ )
\begin{align*}
    (t_1 + t_2 + t_3)\left(\frac{1}{t_1} + \frac{1}{t_2} + \frac{1}{t_3}\right) &= 3 + \frac{a}{b} + \frac{b}{a} + a + \frac{1}{a} + b + \frac{1}{b} 
    &= 3 + \frac{a}{s - a} + \frac{s - a}{a} + a + \frac{1}{a} + s - a + \frac{1}{s - a} 
    &= 2 + s + \frac{a + 1}{s - a} + \frac{s + 1}{a} 
    &= 2 + \frac{a^2 (1 - s) + a(s^2 - s) + (s^2 + s)}{a(s - a)}\text{.}
\end{align*}
Now consider the inequality
\begin{align*}
    a^2 (1 - s) + a(s^2 - s) + (s^2 + s) &\geq 8a(s - a) 
    a^2 (9 - s) + a(s^2 - 9s) + (s^2 + s) &\geq 0\text{.}
\end{align*}
We will show that this always holds. Here the discriminant of the quadratic in  $a$  is \[\Delta = (s^2 - 9s)^2 - 4(9 - s)(s^2 + s) = s(s - 1)(s - 4)(s - 9) \leq 0\text{.}\] Furthermore, the leading coefficient is  $9 - s$  which is always positive so this inequality always holds, as desired.

Now note that since  $0 < a < s$ ,  $a(s - a)$  is positive so we have
\begin{align*}
    2 + \frac{a^2 (1 - s) + a(s^2 - s) + (s^2 + s)}{a(s - a)} &\geq 2 + 8 
    &= 10 
    &= n^2 + 1\text{, a contradiction.}
\end{align*}**Inductive step:** suppose the proposition holds for some fixed  $n \geq 3$ . Let \[S_n = (t_1 + t_2 + \dots + t_n)\left(\frac{1}{t_1} + \frac{1}{t_2} + \dots + \frac{1}{t_n}\right)\text{.}\]
Assume without loss of generality that  $t_1 \leq t_2 \leq t_3 \leq \dots \leq t_{n + 1}$ .

Hence
\begin{align*}
    (n + 1)^2 + 1 &= n^2 + 2n + 2 
    &> S_{n + 1} 
    &= S_n + 1 + \sum_{i = 1}^n \left(\frac{t_i}{t_{n + 1}} + \frac{t_{n + 1}}{t_i}\right) 
    &\geq n^2 + 1 + \sum_{i = 1}^n \left(\frac{t_i}{t_{n + 1}} + \frac{t_{n + 1}}{t_i}\right) 
    2n + 1 &\geq \sum_{i = 1}^n \left(\frac{t_i}{t_{n + 1}} + \frac{t_{n + 1}}{t_i}\right)\text{.}
\end{align*}

Once again let  $a_i = \frac{t_i}{t_{n + 1}}$  for all  $1 \leq i \leq n$ . Suppose that  $t_i + t_j \leq t_{n + 1}$  or equivalently  $a_i + a_j \leq 1$  for some  $i < j$ . We have (where  $s = a_i + a_j \leq 1$ )
\begin{align*}
    a_i + \frac{1}{a_i} + a_j + \frac{1}{a_j} &= s + \frac{1}{a_i} + \frac{1}{s - a_i} 
    &= -\frac{a_i^2 s - a_is^2 - s}{a_i (s - a_i)}\text{.}
\end{align*}
Now consider the inequality
\begin{align*}
    -a_i^2 s + a_is^2 + s &\geq 5a_i (s - a_i) 
    a_i^2 (5 - s) + a_i (s^2 - 5s) + s &\geq 0
\end{align*}
We will show that this always holds. Here the discriminant of the quadratic in  $a$  is \[\Delta = (s^2 - 5s)^2 - 4(5 - s)s = s(s - 1)(s - 4)(s - 5) \leq 0\text{.}\] Furthermore, the leading coefficient is  $5 - s$  which is always positive so this inequality always holds, as desired.

Now note that since  $0 < a_i < s$ ,  $a_i(s - a_i)$  is positive so we have \[-\frac{a_i^2 s - a_is^2 - s}{a_i (s - a_i)} \geq 5\text{.}\]

Thus
\begin{align*}
    \sum_{i = 1}^n \left(\frac{t_i}{t_{n + 1}} + \frac{t_{n + 1}}{t_i}\right) &= \sum_{i = 1}^n \left(a_i + \frac{1}{a_i}\right) 
    &= 5 + \sum_{k \in \{1, 2, \dots, n\} \setminus \{i, j\}} \left(a_k + \frac{1}{a_k}\right) 
    &\geq 5 + 2(n - 2)\text{ (by AM-GM)} 
    &= 2n + 1\text{.}
\end{align*}

Equality holds in the AM-GM part if and only if  $a_k = 1$  for all  $k \in \{1, 2, \dots, n\} \setminus \{i, j\}$  or in other words  $t_k = t_{n + 1}$ . However,  $t_i, t_j \neq 0$  so it follows that  $t_i, t_j < t_{n + 1}$ , and that with the assumption that  $t_1 \leq t_2 \leq \dots \leq t_{n + 1}$ , yields that we must have  $i = 1, j = 2$ . But then by the inductive hypothesis  $t_i + t_j > t_3 = t_4$ , a contradiction.

Thus equality does not hold, and so \[\sum_{i = 1}^n \left(\frac{t_i}{t_{n + 1}} + \frac{t_{n + 1}}{t_i}\right) > 2n + 1\] which is a contradiction.

We first prove the case  $n=3$ . Assume FTSOC we have  $(t_1,t_2,t_3) = (a,b,a+b+k)$ , where  $k \ge 0$ . Then
\begin{align*}
3^2+1 &> (t_1+t_2+t_3)\left(\frac{1}{t_1} + \frac{1}{t_2} + \frac{1}{t_3}\right) 
&\ge 5 + \frac{t_3}{t_1} + \frac{t_3}{t_2} + \frac{t_1}{t_2} + \frac{t_1}{t_3} 
&= 7 + \frac{c+k}{b} + \frac{b+k}{c} + \frac{b+c}{b+c+k} 
&\ge 10 + k\left(\frac 1b + \frac 1c - \frac{1}{b+c+k}\right) 
&\ge 10,
\end{align*}

contradiction. We now show the case  $n \ge 4$ . Assume again FTSOC  $t_1, t_2, t_3$  do not form the sides of a triangle. Then
\begin{align*}
n^2+1 &> \left(t_1 + t_2 + \ldots + t_n\right) \left( \frac{1}{t_1} + \frac{1}{t_2} + \ldots + \frac{1}{t_n} \right) 
&= 10 + (n-3)^2 + \left(t_1+\ldots+t_3\right)\left(\frac{1}{t_4}+\ldots+\frac{1}{t_n}\right) + \left(t_4+\ldots+t_n\right) \left(\frac{1}{t_1}+\ldots+\frac{1}{t_3}\right) 
&\ge 10 + (n-3)^2 + 2\sqrt{10(n-3)^2} 
&= \left(n+\sqrt{10}-3\right)^2 
&\ge n^2+1,
\end{align*}

contradiction.  $\blacksquare$

FTSOC, suppose  $t_1 > t_2 + t_3$ . Therefore, we want to show
\begin{align*}
n^2 + 1 &> \sum_{1 \leq i<j \leq n} \left(\dfrac{t_i}{t_j} + \dfrac{t_j}{t_i}\right) + n 
&= \left(\left(\dfrac{t_1}{t_2}+\dfrac{t_2}{t_1}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_2}{t_3}+\dfrac{t_3}{t_2}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_3}{t_1}+\dfrac{t_1}{t_3}\right)\right) + \sum_{4\leq i<j \leq n} \left(\dfrac{t_i}{t_j} + \dfrac{t_j}{t_i}\right)+n 
&\geq \left(\left(\dfrac{t_1}{t_2}+\dfrac{t_2}{t_1}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_2}{t_3}+\dfrac{t_3}{t_2}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_3}{t_1}+\dfrac{t_1}{t_3}\right)\right) + \left(n^2 - n - 6\right) + n.
\end{align*}
Thus, we want 
\begin{align*}
7 > \left(\dfrac{t_1}{t_2}+\dfrac{t_2}{t_1}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_2}{t_3}+\dfrac{t_3}{t_2}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_3}{t_1}+\dfrac{t_1}{t_3}\right)
\end{align*}
if  $t_1 > t_2 + t_3$ . By AM-GM-HM, note that
\begin{align*}
\left(\dfrac{t_1}{t_2}+\dfrac{t_2}{t_1}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_2}{t_3}+\dfrac{t_3}{t_2}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_3}{t_1}+\dfrac{t_1}{t_3}\right) &= \left(\dfrac{t_2}{t_1} + \dfrac{t_3}{t_1}\right) + \left(\dfrac{t_1}{t_2} + \dfrac{t_1}{t_3}\right) + 2 
&\geq \left(\dfrac{t_2}{t_1} + \dfrac{t_3}{t_1}\right)+ \dfrac{4}{\left(\dfrac{t_1}{t_2} + \dfrac{t_1}{t_3}\right)} + 2 = k + \tfrac{4}{k} + 2.
\end{align*}
Since  $k < 1$ , then we must have that  $k + \tfrac{4}{k} + 2 \leq 7$  which is clearly a contradiction. Thus, for all  $i$ ,  $j$ , and  $k$ , there exists a triangle with side lengths  $t_i$ ,  $t_j$ , and  $t_k$ , as desired.

We will solve the problem by induction on n.

Base case: n=3. We have that:  $$ 10>(a+b+c)(\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{c}) \Leftrightarrow 7abc>\sum{a^2b} $$  WLOG we can assume  $a$  is the largest. Thus we need to show  $a<b+c$ . If we rewrite as a quadratic function in respect to  $a$  (considering  $b$  and  $c$  as parameters) we have:  $$ 0>a^2(b+c)+a(b^2+c^2-7bc)+(b^2c+c^2b)=f(a) $$  We however know:  $$  b+c>a \Leftrightarrow \begin{cases}
  f(b+c)\geq0 
  b+c>\frac{7bc-b^2-c^2}{2(b+c)} 
\end{cases} \Leftrightarrow \begin{cases}
  2(b+c)^3-8(b+c)bc\geq0
  3b^2-3bc+3c^2>0 
\end{cases} \text{ which are both true, finishing the base} $$  IH: for n-1

IS: for n. Let  $A=t_1+t_2+\cdots+t_{n-1}, B=\frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_{n-1}}$ .WLOG we can assume  $t_1\leq t_2 \leq \cdots \leq t_n$ . We know that  $n^2+1>(A+t_n)(B+\frac{1}{t_n}) \Leftrightarrow n^2-Bt_n-\frac{A}{t_n}>AB$ . We will first show that  $Bt_n+\frac{A}{t_n}\geq2n-2$ . This follows from the fact:  $$ Bt_n+\frac{A}{t_n}=\sum_{1\leq i \leq n-1}{\frac{t_n}{t_i}+\frac{t_i}{t_n}} \overset{AM-GM}{\geq} 2n-2 $$  From this it directly follows that  $(n-1)^2+1>AB$ , implying that  $t_i, t_j, t_k$  are the sides of a triangle for  $1\leq i<j<k\leq n-1$ . We are now left to show that  $t_n<t_1+t_2$  from which the desired will follow (because  $t_1, t_2$  are the smallest). For simplicity let  $t_1=x, t_2=y$ . FTSOC let  $t_n=x+y+k$  for some  $k\geq0$ . We know that by Cauchy-Schwarz  $AB\geq(n-1)^2$ . We will now show that  $t_nB+\frac{A}{t_n}\geq 2n-1$ .  $$ t_nB+\frac{A}{t_n}= \frac{t_n}{t_1}+\frac{t_1}{t_n}+\frac{t_2}{t_n}+\frac{t_n}{t_2}+(\frac{t_3}{t_n}+\cdots+\frac{t_{n-1}}{t_n})+(\frac{t_n}{t_3}+\cdots+\frac{t_n}{t_{n-1}}) \overset{\text{AM-GM}}{\geq}  $$     $$  \geq \frac{a+b+k}{a}+\frac{a}{a+b+k}+\frac{a+b+k}{b}+\frac{b}{a+b+k}+2n-6 \geq \frac{a+b}{a}+\frac{a}{a+b}+\frac{a+b}{b}+\frac{b}{a+b}+2n-6= $$    $$ =\frac{(a+b)^2}{ab}+2n-5\overset{\text{AM-GM}}{\geq}2n-1 $$  From here it follows that:  $$ n^2> AB+t_nB+\frac{A}{t_n}\geq n^2 \text{ \Large{ \lightning }} \text{ with which we are done} $$

We show by induction on  $n$  that if there exists at least one triple  $(t_i, t_j, t_k)$  that are not the sides of a triangle, then   $$ n^2+1 \leq (t_1+t_2+\cdots+t_n)\left( \frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_n} \right). $$   $\text{Base Case: }$  For  $n=3,$  we must show that if  $t_1, t_2, t_3$  cannot be the sides of a triangle, then   $$ 10 \leq (t_1+t_2+t_3)\left( \frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\frac{1}{t_3} \right). $$   WLOG, assume that  $t_1 \leq t_2 \leq t_3,$  then we have that  $t_3 > t_1+t_2.$  Also, because our inequality is homogenous, assume that  $t_3=1.$  Letting  $x=t_1+t_2,$  it suffices to show that   $$ 7 \leq \frac{t_1}{t_2}+\frac{t_2}{t_1}+x+\frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}. $$   But by AM-GM,  $\frac{t_1}{t_2}+\frac{t_2}{t_1} \geq 2,$  so it suffices to show that   $$ x+\frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2} \geq 5. $$   But,  $x \leq t_3=1 \implies (x-1)(x-4) \geq 0 \implies 5 \leq x+\frac{4}{x} \leq x+\frac{1}{t_1t_2} \leq x+\frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}$  by  $2$  applications of AM-GM at the end. Therefore, our base case is proven. $\text{Inductive Step: }$  Now, assume that for  $n=k-1 \geq 3$  our proposition holds. Consider the sequence  $t_1, t_2, \cdots t_k$  of positive real numbers. Then if there is at least one triple, say WLOG  $(t_1, t_2, t_3),$  which are not the sides of a triangle then we have by our inductive hypothesis that
\begin{align*}
(t_1+t_2+\cdots+t_k)\left( \frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_k} \right) &=(t_1+t_2+\cdots+t_{k-1})\left( \frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_{k-1}} \right) + \sum_{i=1}^{k-1} \left( \frac{t_i}{t_k}+\frac{t_k}{t_i} \right)+1 
&\geq \left((k-1)^2+1\right)+(k-1)\cdot 2+1 \text{ by AM-GM} 
&= k^2+1.
\end{align*}
Therefore, our induction is complete. QED

Now, for the sake of a contradiction assume that there exists  $t_i, t_j, t_k$  which are not the sides of a triangle, and   $$ n^2+1 > (t_1+t_2+\cdots+t_n)\left( \frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_n} \right). $$   But by our induction above, we have that   $$ n^2+1 \leq (t_1+t_2+\cdots+t_n)\left( \frac{1}{t_1}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_n} \right), $$   a contradiction. Therefore, all  $t_i, t_j, t_k$  are the sides of a triangle. QED

Solved with **Drake Tan**.

Note that
\begin{align*}
(t_1+\cdots+t_n)\left(\frac{1}{t_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_n}\right)&=(t_1+\cdots+t_{n-1})\left(\frac{1}{t_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_{n-1}}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\left(\frac{t_i}{t_n}+\frac{t_n}{t_i}\right)+1
&\geq(t_1+\cdots+t_{n-1})\left(\frac{1}{t_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{t_{n-1}}\right)+2n-1
\end{align*}
and  $n^2+1-(2n-1)=(n-1)^2+1$ , so it suffices to prove the statement for  $n=3$ .

Assume FTSOC WLOG  $t_3=t_1+t_2+a$  for  $a\geq 0$ . Then
\begin{align*}
(t_1+t_2+t_3)\left(\frac{1}{t_2}+\frac{1}{t_2}+\frac{1}{t_3}\right)&=3+\frac{t_1}{t_2}+\frac{t_2}{t_1}+\frac{t_1+t_2}{t_1+t_2+a}+2+\frac{t_2+a}{t_1}+\frac{t_1+a}{t_2}
&\geq 9+\frac{(t_1+t_2)^2}{(t_1+t_2)(t_1+t_2+a)}+\frac{a^2}{at_1}+\frac{a^2}{at_2}
&\geq 9+\frac{(t_1+t_2+2a)^2}{(t_1+t_2)(t_1+t_2+2a)}
&\geq 10,
\end{align*}
a contradiction.  $\square$

