Here is another solution which is more direct (Not elegant in anyway). We use the following notation,  $\angle BAC = \alpha$  ,  $\angle ABC = \beta $  ,   $\angle ACB = \gamma $  ,  $\angle PCB = \gamma_1 \leq \gamma$  ,  $\angle PBC = \beta_1 \leq \beta$ . Now note that \[\angle BPC = \pi - (\gamma_1 + \beta_1) = \pi - \left( \frac{\beta + \gamma}{2} \right) = \frac{\pi}{2} + \frac{\alpha}{2}\] It is easy to see that one can assume WLOG that  $ 2 \gamma_1 \leq \frac{\alpha}{2} + \gamma $ . Now we apply the sine rule in  $ \triangle PBC$  to get \[ PB = \frac{a\sin \gamma_1}{\sin \left( \frac{\pi}{2} + \frac{\alpha}{2}\right )} = \frac{a \sin \gamma_1}{\cos \frac{\alpha}{2}}\] Applying Cosine rule in  $ \triangle ABP$  we get ;\begin{align*}  AP^2 & = PB^2 + AB^2 - 2 PB \cdot AB \cdot \cos ( \beta - \beta_1 )  &= \frac{a^2 \sin^2 \gamma_1}{\cos^2 \frac{\alpha}{2}} + c^2 - 2 \frac{ac \sin \gamma_1 \sin ( \gamma + \frac{\alpha}{2} - \gamma_1)}{\cos \frac{\alpha}{2}} 
& = \frac{a^2 \sin^2 \gamma_1}{\cos^2 \frac{\alpha}{2}} + c^2 - \frac{ac ( \cos ( \gamma + \frac{\alpha}{2} - 2\gamma_1) - \cos (\gamma + \frac{\alpha}{2}))}{\cos \frac{\alpha}{2}} \end{align*} So it suffices to show that this quantity achieves its minimum when  $\gamma_1 = \frac{\gamma}{2}$ . That's a one-variable inequality, which can be done using Calculus, Denoting the latter expression by  $ f(\gamma_1)$  , now \[ f'(x) = \frac{\sin 2x}{\cos^2 \frac{\alpha}{2}} -  \frac{2ac ( \sin ( \gamma + \frac{\alpha}{2} - 2x))}{\cos \frac{\alpha}{2}}  \]. Using Sine Rule in  $\triangle ABC$  and double angle formula we can get,  \[ f'(x)= 0 \implies \frac{a^2 \sin 2x}{ \sin ( \gamma + \frac{\alpha}{2} - 2x)} = \frac{\sin{\gamma}}{\frac{\sin(\alpha}{2})}\]. Letting  $G(2x)$  denote the  $L.H.S$  we have \begin{align*} G'(u) & = \frac{\sin (\gamma + \alpha/2 - u ) \cos(u) + \cos(\gamma + \alpha/2 -u) \sin(u)}{\sin^2 ( \gamma + \alpha/2 - u)} 
& = \frac{\sin(\gamma + \alpha/2)}{\sin^2 ( \gamma + \alpha/2 - u)} > 0 
\end{align*} So  $G$  is injective on  $(0, \gamma + \alpha/2)$ . So  $\gamma/2$  is the only critical point, it is easy to see the minimum is achieved there. 

