\begin{align*}	
	\angle BPC &= 180^{\circ}-(\angle PBC+ \angle PCB) = 180^{\circ} -\angle PBA -\angle PCA &= 180^{\circ} -(180-\angle BAP-\angle APB) - (180^{\circ} - \angle CAP - \angle CPA ) &= \angle A + 360^{\circ} -\angle BPC -180^{\circ} &= 180^{\circ}+\angle A - \angle BPC 
\end{align*}
Then,  $2\angle BPC = 180^{\circ} + \angle A \implies \angle BPC = 90^{\circ} + \angle \frac12 A.$ Also, we get that $\angle BIC = 90^{\circ} + \angle  \frac12 A $ Then  $\angle BPC = \angle BIC,$  hence  $BCPI$  is cyclic. Let  $AI$  meet  $\odot ABC$  at  $X,$  from fact 5, we know that  $IX$  is the radius of  $BCPI,$  and  $A, I, X$  are collinear. So trivially,  $AP + PX \geq AX = AI + IX \implies AP \geq AI$  with equality only when  $P=I.$

