We can write that \[\dfrac{[RPK]}{[RQL]} = \dfrac{\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot RP\cdot PK\sin(\angle RPK)}{\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot RQ\cdot QL\sin(\angle RQL)} = \dfrac{RP\cdot PK}{RQ\cdot QL} = \dfrac{RP\cdot BC}{RQ\cdot AC}.\] Now, since  $\triangle ARB$  is isosceles, we know that \[RA = \dfrac{AB}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}.\] Hence, by Ptolemy, we have that \[RA\cdot BC+RB\cdot AC = RA(BC+AC) = RC\cdot AB.\] So, \[RC = \dfrac{BC+AC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}.\] Finally, 
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{RP}{RQ} &= \dfrac{RC-PC}{RC-QC} [0.5em]
&= \dfrac{\dfrac{BC+AC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}-\dfrac{KC}{\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}}{\dfrac{BC+AC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}-\dfrac{LC}{\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}} [0.5em]
&= \dfrac{\dfrac{BC+AC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}-\dfrac{BC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}}{\dfrac{BC+AC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}-\dfrac{AC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}} [0.5em]
&= \dfrac{\dfrac{AC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}}{\dfrac{BC}{2\cos\left(\tfrac{\angle C}{2}\right)}} [0.5em]
&= \dfrac{AC}{BC}.
\end{align*}

Hence, \[\dfrac{[RPK]}{[RQL]} = \dfrac{RP\cdot BC}{RQ\cdot AC} = \dfrac{AC\cdot BC}{BC\cdot AC} = 1\] so we are done.  $\square$

I really like the official "normal" solution (if almost everyone preferred the comb null solution, then I doubt this is that normal) . In any case, here we go.
-----------------------
Define  $P$  in the same way as everyone else, while notice that  $P(0, 0, 0) \neq 0$ , and assume that  $\deg{P} < 3n$ . Let  $P(x, y, z) = \sum_{\alpha+\beta+\gamma < 3n}p_{\alpha,\beta,\gamma}x^{\alpha}y^{\beta}z^{\gamma}$ . Now consider the sum
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=0}^{n} \sum_{j=0}^{n} \sum_{k=0}^{n} (-1)^{i+j+k} 
\binom{n}{i} \binom{n}{j} \binom{n}{k} \sum_{\alpha+\beta+\gamma \leq N} 
p_{\alpha,\beta,\gamma} i^{\alpha} j^{\beta} k^{\gamma}
\end{align*}
Notice this has to evaluate to  $P(0, 0 ,0) \neq 0$  as every other term is  $0$ . However, turns out that you can compute this as
\[\sum_{\alpha+\beta+\gamma \leq N} \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i}\binom{n}{i}i^{\alpha}\right) \left( \sum_{j=1}^{n}(-1)^{j}\binom{n}{j}j^{\beta} \right) \left( \sum_{k=1}^{n}(-1)^{k}\binom{n}{k}k^{\gamma} \right)\]
which is always  $0$ , because one of  $\alpha$ ,  $\beta$ ,  $\gamma$  will be less than  $n$ , say it is  $\alpha$ , and so is well known  $\sum_{i=1}^{n}(-1)^{i}\binom{n}{i}i^{\alpha} = 0$ . Which is a contradiction.  $\Box$

Select  $p \equiv 1 \pmod 8$  with  $p \ge 2013$ .  We will show that there exists an  $n$  such that  $p \ge 2n+\sqrt{2n}$  and  $n^2 \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$ .  This is sufficient because we can generate arbitrarily large solutions by taking  $p$  large, as  $p \mid n^2+1 \implies n \ge \sqrt{p-1}$ .

In fact, we will select  $n$  such that  $n \le \frac 12 (p-h)$  where  $h = \left\lceil \sqrt p \right\rceil$ ; in that case it is not hard to verify  $p \ge 2n + \sqrt{2n}$ .  

Because  $p \equiv 1 \pmod 4$  there exists some  $\frac 12 p < x < p$  such that  $x^2 \equiv -1 \pmod p$ .  Now set  $x = \frac{p+1}{2} + t$ .  We need only show  $t \ge \frac{h-1}{2}$  because then  $n = p-x$  will work.  Assume for contradiction this is false; then \begin{align*}
		0 \equiv &= 4(x^2+1) \pmod{p} 
		&= \left( p+1+2t \right)^2 + 4 
		&\equiv (2t+1)^2 + 4 \pmod{p} 
		&< h^2+4
	\end{align*} So we have that  $(2t+1)^2+4$  is positive and divisible by  $p$ , yet at most  $\left\lceil \sqrt{p} \right\rceil^2 + 4 < 2p$ .  So it must be the case that  $(2t+1)^2+4 = p$ , but this has no solutions modulo  $8$ .

......

Pick some prime  $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$ . There are two solutions to  $n^2 \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$  that add up to  $p$ , hence one must be less than  $\tfrac{p-1}{2}:=a$ . Let the smaller one be  $a-k$ . Note that  $(a-k)^2 \equiv -1 \pmod{p}$  translates to  $(2k+1)^2 \equiv -4 \pmod{p}$ . This means  $$ (2k+1)^2 \geq p-4 \iff p \leq 4k^2+4k+5. $$  On the other hand, if  $p\leq 2n+\sqrt{2n}$ , then
\begin{align*}
p &\leq 2(a-k)+\sqrt{2(a-k)}
2a+1 &\leq 2(a-k)+\sqrt{2(a-k)}
2k+1 &\leq \sqrt{2(a-k)}
4k^2+6k+2 &\leq 2a+1
p &\geq 4k^2+6k+2,
\end{align*}
which is a contradiction for sufficiently large  $k$ . On the other hand, if  $k$  stays bounded then  $p$  stays bounded as well, since  $(2k+1)^2+4$  has finitely many prime divisors for each  $k$ , which is impossible by Dirichlet. Thus for all sufficiently large  $p$ , picking  $n$  as described will make  $n^2+1$  have a prime divisor (namely  $p$ ) greater than  $2n+\sqrt{2n}$ .  $\blacksquare$

For each sufficiently large prime  $p \equiv 1 \pmod{4}$ , let  $n$  be the minimal root of  $n^2 + 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ . I claim that this  $n$  has  $p > 2n + \sqrt{2n}$ , which will finish. Indeed, clearly  $n \le \frac{p-1}{2} = a$ , so let  $n = a - k$ . Now 
\begin{align*}
  n^2 + 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p} &\implies (a - k)^2 + 1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p} 
  &\implies (2k+1)^2 + 4 \equiv 0 \pmod{p} 
  &\implies p \le 4k^2 + 4k + 5. 
\end{align*}
Putting this back in terms of  $n$  gives 
\begin{align*}
  p \le 4k^2 + 4k + 5  &\implies p \le (p - 2n)^2 + 4 
  &\implies p^2 - (4n+1)p + (4n^2+4) \ge 0 
  &\implies p \ge \frac{4n + 1 + \sqrt{8n - 15}}{2}
\end{align*}
which implies the bound for sufficiently large  $n$ .

<details><summary>A small remark on motivation</summary>I think the main motivation here is that getting direct bounds on  $p$  is pretty annoying (how do we get  $2n + \sqrt{2n}$  directly from  $n^2 + 1$ ? This would be extremely unnatural), so this is not worth trying out. Instead, considering  $p$  first and trying to bound the smallest value of  $n$  that follows is much more reasonable. In addition, the bound  $n \le \frac{p-1}{2}$  is immediate but not what we want, so we expect this is only sharp for finitely many  $n$ . Indeed, 
\begin{align*}
  \left( \frac{p-1}{2} \right)^2 + 1 &\equiv 0 \pmod{p} \implies p = 5 
  \left( \frac{p-1}{2} - 1 \right)^2 + 1 &\equiv 0 \pmod{p} \implies p \le 13
\end{align*}
which gives the idea to generalize to  $n = \frac{p-1}{2} - k$  pretty easily.</details>

