Let  $M, N, P$  be the midpoints of  $BC, CA, AB$ . Let  $X = \Gamma_B \cap \Gamma_C \neq H$ , define  $Y, Z$  similarly. Then  $XH \perp NP$  and  $AH \perp NP$  so we have  $XH || AH \Rightarrow A, X, H$  is a line. Similarly,  $B, Y, H$  and  $C, Z, H$  are collinear. Consider the circle passing through  $A_1, A_2, B_1$  and let it intersect  $\Gamma_B$  at  $B_2'$ . Then, by radical axis, we have  $HZ, A_1A_2, B_1B_2'$  are concurrent at  $C$ , so  $B_1B_2'$  must pass through  $C$ , hence  $B_2' = B_2$ . We can thus conclude that  $A_1A_2B_1B_2$  is cyclic; denote the circumcircle  $R_C$ . Define  $R_A, R_B$  similarly. If we have any two of these being the same circle, then we are done. Otherwise, suppose they are different. Then by radical axis again,  $AB, BC, CA$  must be concurrent, a contradiction. Therefore,  $A_1A_2B_1B_2C_1C_2$  is cyclic.

