[/asy]**<span style="color:red">Claim:**</span>  $A_1A_2C_1C_2$ ,  $B_1B_2C_1C_2$  and  $A_1A_2B_1B_2$  are cyclic quadrilaterals.

*Proof:* We will only  $A_1A_2B_1B_2$  is cyclic because others will follow symmetrically. Applying ``Apollonius Theorem'' on  $\triangle HBC$  with respect to the  $H-$ median using the fact  $\overline{BH} = 2R\cos(B)$  and  $a = 2R\sin(A)$  we get
\begin{align*}
    M_aH^2 = 2R^2 \left(\cos^2(C)+\cos^2(B)-\frac{\sin^2(A)}{2}\right) = M_aA_1^2.
\end{align*}
We want to show the cyclicity by Power of a Point. Observe
\begin{align*}
    CA_2 \cdot CA_1 = (CM_a - M_aA_2)(CM_a + MA_1) = CM_a^2 - M_aA_1^2 
    CB_2 \cdot CB_1 = (CM_b - M_bB_2)(CM_b + MB_1) = CM_b^2 - M_bB_1^2
\end{align*}
Therefore it suffices us to show that  $CM_a^2 - M_aA_1^2 = CM_b^2 - M_bB_1^2$ . Note that
\begin{align*}
    CM_a^2 - M_aA_1^2 = \frac{a^2}{4} - 2R^2\left(\cos^2(C)+\cos^2(B)-\frac{\sin^2(A)}{2}\right) 
    CM_b^2 - M_bB_1^2 = \frac{b^2}{4} - 2R^2\left(\cos^2(A)+\cos^2(C)-\frac{\sin^2(B)}{2}\right)
\end{align*}
The following calculation will show that both of them are actually equal where we will substitute  $a = 2R\sin(A)$  and  $b = 2R\sin(B)$  to get the whole thing completely in terms of trigonometric functions.
\begin{align*}
    \frac{a^2}{4} - 2R^2\left(\cos^2(C)+\cos^2(B)-\frac{\sin^2(A)}{2}\right) & = \frac{b^2}{4} - 2R^2\left(\cos^2(A)+\cos^2(C)-\frac{\sin^2(B)}{2}\right) 
    \iff \sin^2(A) - 2\left(\cos^2(B)-\frac{\sin^2(A)}{2}\right)             & = \sin^2(B) - 2\left(\cos^2(A)- \frac{\sin^2(B)}{2}\right)                 
    \iff 2(\sin^2(A)-\cos^2(B))                                              & = 2(\sin^2(B) - \cos^2(A))                                                 
    \iff \sin^2(A) + \cos^2(A)                                               & = \sin^2(B) + \cos^2(B)
\end{align*}
which is always true, so we are done!  $\square$ ----------
The finish to the problem from here on is easy. One can conclude it with radical axis by considering the radical axis of  $\odot(A_1A_2B_1C_2)$ ,  $\odot(B_1B_2C_1C_2)$  and  $\odot(A_1A_2B_1B_2)$ . Another way is to just show  $O$  is the center of all the centers which is just true because  $O$  lies on pretty much on all perpendicular bisectors.  $\blacksquare$

Notice that  $H$  lies on the radical axis of  $\Gamma_A$  and  $\Gamma_B$ . It is also known that the radical axis is perpendicular to the line connecting the centers. Thus,  $C$  lies on the radical axis of  $\Gamma_A$  and  $\Gamma_B$ . 

This means that by definition of the radical axis, we have

\[CB_1 \cdot CB_2 = CA_1 \cdot CA_2\]

which means that  $A_1, A_2, B_1, B_2$  are concyclic. We can show that  $A_1, A_2, C_1, C_2$  are concyclic making all six points concylic.  $\square$ [asy]
