Let  $\omega_a$ ,  $\omega_b$  and  $\omega_c$  be the circles. Note that  $\overline{AH}$  is the radical axis of  $\omega_b$  and  $\omega_c$  as if  $M$  and  $N$  are the midpoints of  $\overline{AB}$  and  $\overline{BC}$  we have  $\overline{AH} \perp \overline{MN}$  and  $H \in \omega_b, \omega_c$ . Then by power of a point,
\begin{align*}
AB_1 \cdot AB_2 = AC_1 \cdot AC_2 \implies B_1B_2C_1C_2 \text{ is cyclic}
\end{align*}
Similarly we may conclude  $A_1A_2B_1B_2$  and  $A_1A_2C_1C_2$  are cyclic. Now assume the circles of the cyclic quads are distinct. Then their radical axes are  $\overline{AB}$ ,  $\overline{BC}$  and  $\overline{AC}$  which do not concur nor are all parallel so done. $\square$

