Let  $ P(\bar{x}) = (x_1 + x_2 + ... + x_{2n})^k$  which has the usual multinomial expansion.  To compute  $ M$ , we want to compute the sum of the coefficients of the terms where the powers of  $ x_1, x_2, ... x_n$  are odd and the powers of  $ x_{n + 1}, x_{n + 2}, ... x_{2n}$  are zero.  Recall that the sum of the odd terms of a polynomial  $ Q(x)$  is given by  $ \frac {Q(1) - Q( - 1)}{2}$  (in other words, a second roots-of-unity filter).  Applying this filter over  $ x_1, x_2, ..., x_n$  gives
 $ M = \frac {1}{2^n} \sum_{i = 0}^{n} ( - 1)^i {n \choose i} (n - 2i)^k$ 

where we have chosen  $ i$  of the variables to be  $ - 1$  and  $ n - i$  of the variables to be  $ 1$  in each term.  To compute  $ N$ , we want to compute the sum of the coefficients of the terms where the powers of  $ x_1, x_2, ... x_n$  are odd and the powers of  $ x_{n + 1}, x_{n + 2}, ..., x_{2n}$  are even.  Recall that the sum of the even terms of a polynomial  $ Q(x)$  is given by  $ \frac {Q(1) + Q( - 1)}{2}$ .  Applying the first filter over  $ x_1, x_2, ..., x_n$  and the second over  $ x_{n + 1}, x_{n + 2}, ..., x_{2n}$  gives

\begin{eqnarray*} N & = & \frac {1}{2^n} \sum_{j = 0}^{n} {n \choose j} \left( \frac {1}{2^n} \sum_{i = 0}^{n} ( - 1)^i {n \choose i} (n - 2i + n - 2j)^k \right) 
& = & \frac {1}{2^{2n}} \sum_{s = 0}^{2n} (2n - 2s)^k \sum_{i + j = s} ( - 1)^i {n \choose i} {n \choose j} 
& = & \frac {2^k}{2^{2n}} \sum_{r = 0}^{n} (n - 2r)^k ( - 1)^r {n \choose r} 
& = & 2^{k - n} M 
\end{eqnarray*}

where  $ r = \frac {s}{2}$  and we note that  $ \sum_{i + j = s} ( - 1)^k {n \choose i} {n \choose j} = [x^s](x - 1)^n (x + 1)^n = [x^s] (x^2 - 1)^n$ .  Interestingly enough, neither of these expressions makes it particularly clear that they evaluate to zero when  $ k < n$ .  (The generalization when the lamps are not assumed to be binary but take on lighting values  $ \bmod m$  is given by the [Dirichlet characters](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_character)  $ \bmod m$ .)

We will calculate  $N$  first. Let  $b_1,\ldots,b_{2n}$  be the number of times each lamp was switched, so that  $b_1+\cdots+b_{2n}=k$ . Since  $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ 's are all odd, and  $b_{n+1},\ldots,b_{2n}$  are even, let  $b_i=2a_i+1$  for  $1\le i\le n$  and let  $b_i=2a_i$  for  $n+1\le i \le 2n$ , so  $a_i\ge 0$ . Then  $2(a_1+\cdots+a_{2n})+n=k$ , so  $a_1+\cdots+a_{2n} =\frac{k-n}{2}$ . Therefore,  $N$  is  $k!$  times the coefficient of  $X^k$  in
\begin{align*}
    F(X) &= \sum_{k'\ge 0} \sum_{a_1+\cdots+a_{2n}=\frac{k'-n}{2}} \frac{k'!}{(2a_1+1)!\cdots (2a_n+1)! (2a_{n+1})! \cdots (2a_{2n})!} \cdot \frac{X^{k'}}{k'!}  
    &= \sum_{k'\ge 0} \sum_{a_1+\cdots +a_{2n} =\frac{k'-n}{2}} \frac{X^{k'}}{(2a_1+1)!\cdots (2a_n+1)! (2a_{n+1})! \cdots (2a_{2n})!} 
    &=  \sum_{k'\ge 0} \sum_{a_1+\cdots +a_{2n} =\frac{k'-n}{2}} \frac{X^{2a_1+1}\cdots X^{2a_n+1}}{(2a_1+1)!\cdots (2a_n+1)!}  \frac{X^{2a_{n+1}} \cdots X^{2a_{2n}}}{(2a_{n+1})! \cdots (2a_{2n})!}  
    &= \left( \sum_{X_i \text{ odd}} \frac{X^{X_1+\cdots+X_n}}{X_1!\cdots X_n!} \right) \left(\sum_{y_i \text{ even}} \frac{X^{y_1+\cdots+y_n}}{y_1!\cdots y_n!} \right) \qquad (\text{where } X_i=2a_i+1, y_i=2a_{n+i}) 
    &= \left(1+\frac{X^2}{2!}+\frac{X^4}{4!} + \cdots \right)^n \left( X+\frac{X^3}{3!}+\frac{X^5}{5!} + \cdots \right)^n 
    &= \left(\frac{e^X+e^{-X}}{2} \right)^n \left( \frac{e^X-e^{-X}}{2} \right)^n 
    &= \frac{1}{2^n} \left(\frac{e^{2X}-e^{-2X}}{2}\right)^n.
\end{align*} 
For  $M$ , it's the same as  $N$  but we don't need the  $y_i$ 's, so  $M$  is  $k!$  times the coefficient of  $X^k$  in 
\[ \left(X+\frac{X^3}{3!}+\frac{X^5}{5!}+\cdots\right)^n= \left(\frac{e^X-e^{-X}}{2}\right)^n.\]
So the answer is 
\[ \frac{1}{2^n} \cdot \frac{\left(\frac{e^{2X}-e^{-2X}}{2}\right)^n [X^k]}{\left(\frac{e^{X}-e^{-X}}{2}\right)^n[X^k]} = \frac{1}{2^n} \cdot \frac{C(2X)^k}{CX^k} = \boxed{2^{k-n}}\]
for some constant  $C$  we don't care about.

