Now I will show  $n^2-n+1$  works. Define an  $A$ -starting station to be one with no cable cars from station  $A$  leading into it, and define an  $A$ -ending station logically; also define a  $B$ -starting station and  $B$ -ending station similarly. Next, define an  $A$ -chain of stations to be a nonempty  $S_1,S_2,\ldots,S_m$  where  $S_1 \to S_2\to \cdots \to S_m$ ,  $S_1$  is  $A$ -starting, and  $S_m$  is  $A$ -ending. Note that any vertex with indegree and outdegree zero is its own chain. Define a  $B$ -chain similarly. Observe that, in general, for  $n^2-x$  cable cars on  $n^2$  stations, there can exist at most  $x$  chains in that company (otherwise after connecting  $x-1$  ending stations to a different starting station each, we would have  $n^2-1$  vertices but a non-connected graph). So, for  $n^2-n+1$ , there are at most  $n-1$   $A$ -chains and  $n-1$   $B$ -chains. Hence by Pigeonhole we must have a  $B$ -chain with at least  $\lceil\tfrac{n^2}{n-1}\rceil\geq n+2$  stations in it. Let  $C$  be such a chain. Then, by Pigeonhole, it must intersect at least one of the  $n-1$   $A$ -chains at least twice. But if two chains from different companies intersect at stations  $X,Y$  and  $X$  is lower than  $Y$ , then by definition  $X$  is linked to  $Y$  by both companies, so when  $k=n^2-n+1$  there are always two stations linked by both companies, as desired.  $\blacksquare$ **Remark:** I think this should work with "one-station chain" issues. Also note that we only need there to be a  $B$ -chain with at least  $n$  stations, which is somewhat surprising as it means the bound for  $n^2-n+1$  is rather "loose", but for  $n^2-n$  it fails!

