Guess this time I nailed it, @shalomrav, but of course there could always be another mistake... If someone would be kind enough to publish the LaTex code so that every one can read, it would be great. Here we go:

_________________________________________________________________________________________________________

Let  $P_i$  be the sequence formed by the ordered weight of the four stones coloured  $i$ :  $P_i=(a_i,b_i,c_i,d_i)$  and  $a_i<b_i<c_i<d_i$  . Let also  $x_i = b_i-a_i$ ;  $y_i = c_i-b_i$ ;  $z_i=d_i-c_i$ . Call  $S$  all the sums of these groups, such that  $ S_a = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i $ ;  $S_b = \sum_{i=1}^n b_i$  ;  $S_c = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i $ ;  $S_d = \sum_{i=1}^n d_i $ ;  $S_x = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i $ ;  $S_y = \sum_{i=1}^n y_i $ ;  $S_z = \sum_{i=1}^n z_i$ It's trivial that  $S_x = S_b - S_a$  ;  $S_y = S_c - S_b$  ;  $S_z = S_d - S_c$  . We also have  $S_x + S_y = S_c - S_a$  and  $S_x + S_y + S_z = S_d - S_a$  . Adding up the first and the last two equations, we get
 \begin {align*} 3S_x + 2S_y + S_z &= S_a + S_b + S_c + S_d - 4S_a &\Rightarrow 
  3S_x + 2S_y + S_z  &= 2n \cdot (4n+1)  - 4S_a &\Rightarrow 
  2 \cdot (S_x + S_y) + S_x + S_z &= 2 \cdot (n(4n+1) - 2S_a) &\Rightarrow 
  S_x+S_z &\equiv 0 \,\,\,\, (mod 2) 
  \end{align*}
Straightforward calculation also yields  $ S_a+S_d \equiv S_b+S_c \,\, (mod 2)$  and  $S_x \equiv S_z \,\, (mod2) $  
Now, if  $S_a+S_d = S_b+S_c$ , problem solved. Just take   $$  A = \bigcup \{a_i, d_i\} \text{  and  } B = \bigcup \{b_i, c_i\} \text{, whose sums are equal.} $$  We may assume then  $S_a+S_d \neq S_b+S_c \Rightarrow S_x \neq S_z$ . Let  $\alpha_i = x_i - z_i = (b_i-a_i)-(d_i-c_i)=(b_i+c_i)-(a_i+d_i)$ .

We'll prove that there is a subset  $T_{half}$  of  $\{ 1 \le i \le n, \alpha_i\neq 0\}$ , such that  $\sum_{i\in T_{half}} \alpha_i = \frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$ . 

Suppose, by absurdity,  $T_{half}$  doesn't exist. For any  $T$ , call  $S_T = \sum_{i\in T}\alpha_i$  and order the sequence  $(S_{T_0}, S_{T_1}, \dots, S_{T_N})$  with the sums of all subsets  $T$  of  $V = \{ 1 \le i \le n, \alpha_i\neq 0\}$ . Take  $k<l \in V$  such that  $S_{T_k}$  and  $S_{T_l}$  are consecutive elements of the ordered sequence and   $$ S_{T_k} < \frac{S_z-S_x}{2} < S_{T_l} $$   
Consider  $\alpha_j>0$  the least natural such that  $j \in T_l$  and  $j \notin T_k$ . Of course there is such  $j$ , as  $S_{T_l}>S_{T_k}$ . Now, if  $T_m = T_k \cup \{\alpha_j\}$ , we have  $S_{T_m}=S_{T_k}+\alpha_j>S_{T_k}$ . Since  $j \in T_l$  by construction,  $S_{T_m} \ge S_{T_l}$ . But remember every  $\alpha_i$  is positive. So if  $S_{T_m} > S_{T_l}$ , then there would be  $j' \in V$ , such that  $S_{T_k} +\alpha_{j'} = S_{T_l} \Rightarrow \alpha_{j'} < \alpha_j \; (i)$ , a contradiction. We may deduce then that  $T_l = T_m = T_k \cup \{j\}$  and  $S_{T_l} = S_{T_k} + \alpha_j \; (ii)$ . 

Let  $\bar{T_i}$  be the complement of  $T_i$  in  $V$  and  $\bar{S_{T_i}} = \sum_{i\in \bar{T_i}}\alpha_i$ . We have  $S_{T_i}+\bar{S_{T_i}} = S_z-S_x$ . So  $\bar{S_{T_l}} = \bar{S_{T_k}} - \alpha_j$ By the absurdity hypothesis,  $S_{T_l}>\frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$ , so  $2S_{T_l} > S_{T_l}+\bar{S_{T_l}} \Rightarrow S_{T_l} > \bar{S_{T_l}}$ . Also by the same hypothesis, we have  $S_{T_k}<\frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$ , so  $2S_{T_k} < S_{T_k}+\bar{S_{T_k}} \Rightarrow S_{T_k} < \bar{S_{T_k}}$ , which implies  $\bar{S_{T_k}} \ge S_{T_l} \; (iii)$ .

Substituting  $(ii)$  in  $(iii)$ , we get:  $\bar{S_{T_k}} \ge S_{T_k} + \alpha_j \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_k}} - S_{T_k} \ge \alpha_j$ . Also by the hypothesis,  $\bar{S_{T_l}} \le S_{T_k} \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_k}} - \alpha_j \le S_{T_k} \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_k}} - S_{T_k} \le \alpha_j$ . So  $\bar{S_{T_k}} - S_{T_k} = \alpha_j$  and  $T_l = \bar{T_k}$ .

But we know that  $\bar{S_{T_k}} > S_{T_k}$ , thus  $\bar{S_{T_k}} \ge S_{T_l} \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_k}} \ge S_{T_k} + \alpha_j \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_l}} + \alpha_j \ge S_{T_k} + \alpha_j \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_l}} \ge S_{T_k}$ . So, always by the absurdity hypothesis:
\begin{align*}
S_{T_l} &\le \bar{S_{T_l}} &\Rightarrow 
S_{T_l} - \bar{S_{T_l}} &\le 0 &\Rightarrow 
S_{T_k} + \alpha_j - (\bar{S_{T_k}} - \alpha_j) &\le 0 &\Rightarrow 
\bar{S_{T_k}} - S_{T_k} &\ge 2\alpha_j &\Rightarrow 
\alpha_j &\ge 2\alpha_j &\Rightarrow \alpha_j \le 0 \qquad \text{(contradiction!)}
\end{align*}
The contradiction warrants our proof. Take then the subset  $T_{half}$  of  $V$ , such that  $\sum_{i\in T_{half}} \alpha_i = \frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$ . Obviously  $\sum_{i\in \bar{T_{half}}} \alpha_i = \frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$  as well.

We begin partition the stones in two groups,  $A_1$  and  $B_1$ , defined as follows:
\begin {align*}
A_1 = \{a_i, d_i | i \in T_{half}\} \cup \{b_i, c_i | i \notin T_{half} \} 
B_1 = \{b_i, c_i | i \in T_{half}\} \cup \{a_i, d_i | i \notin T_{half} \}
\end {align*}
Each group has exactly two stones of each colour  $i$ . If we calculate the weight difference between the groups, we get:
\begin {align*}
S_{A_1}-S_{B_1} & = \sum_{i \in T_{half}} (a_i+d_i) + \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} (b_i+c_i) - \sum_{i \in T_{half}} (b_i+c_i) - \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} (a_i+d_i) 
&= \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} {(b_i+c_i)-(a_i+d_i)} - \sum_{i \in T_{half}} {(b_i+c_i) - (a_i+d_i)} 
&= \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} \alpha_i - \sum_{i \in T_{half}} \alpha_i 
&= \frac{S_z-S_x}{2} - \frac{S_z-S_x}{2} 
&= 0
\end {align*}
For the stones such that  $\alpha_i = 0$ , the solution is similar, only the choice is different. Let  $V'=\{ 1 \le i \le n, \alpha_i= 0\}$ ,  $S_x' = \sum_{i \in V'} {x_i}$ ,  $S_z' = \sum_{i \in V'} {z_i}$  and  $S_{xz}' = \sum_{i \in V'} {(x_i+z_i)}$ . We'll prove there is a subset  $T_{half}'$  of  $V'$ , such that  $\sum_{i\in T_{half}'}{x_i} = \frac{S_{xz}'}{2}$ . Remember   $S_x+S_z \equiv 0 \,\, (mod 2)$  and, for  $i \in V'$ ,  $x_i=z_i$ , so  $S_{xz}'$  is even, and equal to  $2S_x'$ . Now, since every  $x_i$  and  $z_i$  are positive, the demonstration is identical to the one for  $\alpha_i>0$ , with  $x$  instead of  $\alpha$ .
We can now partition the remaining stones in two other groups,  $A_2$  and  $B_2$ , defined as follows:
\begin {align*}
A_2 = \{a_i, c_i | i \in T_{half}'\} \cup \{b_i, d_i | i \notin T_{half}' \} 
B_2 = \{b_i, d_i | i \in T_{half}'\} \cup \{a_i, c_i | i \notin T_{half}' \}
\end {align*}
Each group has also exactly two stones of each colour  $i$ . If we calculate the weight difference between the groups, we get:
\begin {align*}
S_{A_2}-S_{B_2} & = \sum_{i \in T_{half}'} (a_i+c_i) + \sum_{i \notin T_{half}'} (b_i+d_i) - \sum_{i \in T_{half}'} (b_i+d_i) - \sum_{i \notin T_{half}'} (a_i+c_i) 
&= \sum_{i \notin T_{half}'} {(b_i+d_i)-(a_i+c_i)} - \sum_{i \in T_{half}'} {(b_i+d_i) - (a_i+c_i)} 
&= \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} (x_i+z_i) - \sum_{i \in T_{half}} (x_i+z_i) 
&= \frac{S_{xz}'}{2} - \frac{S_{xz}'}{2} 
&= 0
\end {align*}
Making  $A = A1 \cup A2$  and  $B = B1 \cup B2$  solves the problem.  $\hfill \blacksquare$

