Let  $P_i$  be the sequence formed by the ordered weight of the four stones coloured  $i$ :  $P_i=(a_i,b_i,c_i,d_i)$  and  $a_i<b_i<c_i<d_i$  . Let also  $x_i = b_i-a_i$ ;  $y_i = c_i-d_i$ ;  $z_i=d_i-c_i$ . Call  $S$  all the sums of these groups, such that  $ S_a = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i $ ;  $S_b = \sum_{i=1}^n b_i$  ;  $S_c = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i $ ;  $S_d = \sum_{i=1}^n d_i $ ;  $S_x = \sum_{i=1}^n x_i $ ;  $S_y = \sum_{i=1}^n y_i $ ;  $S_z = \sum_{i=1}^n z_i$ It's trivial that  $S_x = S_b - S_a$  ;  $S_y = S_c - S_b$  ;  $S_z = S_d - S_c$  . We also have  $S_x + S_y = S_c - S_a$  and  $S_x + S_y + S_z = S_d - S_a$  . Adding up the first and the last two equations, we get
 \begin {align*} 3S_x + 2S_y + S_z &= S_a + S_b + S_c + S_d - 4S_a &\Rightarrow 
  3S_x + 2S_y + S_z  &= 2n \cdot (4n+1)  - 4S_a &\Rightarrow 
  2 \cdot (S_x + S_y) + S_x + S_z &= 2 \cdot (n(4n+1) - 2S_a) &\Rightarrow 
  S_x+S_z &\equiv 0 \,\,\,\, (mod 2) 
  \end{align*}
Straightforward calculation also yields  $ S_a+S_d \equiv S_b+S_c \,\, (mod 2)$  and  $S_x \equiv S_z \,\, (mod2) $  
Now, if  $S_a+S_d = S_b+S_c$ , problem solved. Just take   $$  A = \bigcup \{a_i, d_i\} \text{  and  } B = \bigcup \{b_i, c_i\} \text{, whose sums are equal.} $$  We may assume then  $S_a+S_d \neq S_b+S_c \Rightarrow S_x \neq S_z$ . Let  $\alpha_i = x_i - z_i = (b_i-a_i)-(d_i-c_i)=(b_i+c_i)-(a_i+d_i)$ . We'll prove that there is a subset  $T_{half}$  of  $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$ , such that  $\sum_{i\in T_{half}} \alpha_i = \frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$ . 

Suppose, by absurdity,  $T_{half}$  doesn't exist. For any  $T$ , call  $S_T = \sum_{i\in T}\alpha_i$  and order the sequence  $(S_{T_0}, S_{T_1}, \dots, S_{T_N})$  with the sums of all subsets  $T$ . Take  $k \in \{0,1, \dots, n-1\}$  such that   $$ S_{T_k} < \frac{S_z-S_x}{2} < S_{T_{k+1}} $$  Consider  $\alpha_j>0$  the least natural such that  $j \in T_k+1$  and  $j \notin T_k$ . Of course there is such  $j$ , since  $S_{T_{k+1}}>S_{T_k}$ . Now, if  $T_l = T_k \cup \{\alpha_j\}$ , we have  $S_{T_l}=S_{T_k}+\alpha_j>S_{T_k}\Rightarrow T_{k+1} = T_l \text{ (by construction) } \Rightarrow T_{k+1} = T_k \cup \{j\}$ .

Let  $\bar{T_i}$  be the complement of  $T_i$  in  $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$  and  $\bar{S_{T_i}} = \sum_{i\in \bar{T_i}}\alpha_i$ . We have  $S_{T_i}+\bar{S_{T_i}} = S_z-S_x$ . Since  $S_{T_{k+1}}>\frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$ , we have also:
\begin {align*}
2S_{T_{k+1}} > S_{T_k}+\bar{S_{T_k}} &\Rightarrow 
2 (S_{T_k}+\alpha_j)> S_{T_k} + \bar{S_{T_k}} &\Rightarrow 
\bar{S_{T_k}}-S_{T_k}< 2{\alpha_j}  &(i)
\end {align*}

By the absurdity hypothesis,  $\bar{S_{T_k}}>S_{T_k}\Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_k}}\ge S_{T_{k+1}}\Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_k}}\ge S_{T_k}+\alpha_j \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_{k+1}}} + \alpha_j \ge S_{T_k}+\alpha_j \Rightarrow \bar{S_{T_{k+1}}} \ge S_{T_k} $ . So we have the following inequalities:
\begin {align*}
S_{T_k} &\le \bar{S_{T_{k+1}}} < \bar{S_{T_k}} \quad and 
S_{T_k} &< S_{T_{k+1}} \le \bar{S_{T_k}}
\end {align*}
As, always by our the absurdity hypothesis,  $ S_{T_{k+1}} \le \bar{S_{T_{k+1}}}$ , both inequalities yield:
\begin {align*}
S_{T_k} < S_{T_{k+1}} \le \bar{S_{T_{k+1}}} < \bar{S_{T_k}} &\Rightarrow 
S_{T_k} + \alpha_j \le \bar{S_{T_k}} - \alpha_j &\Rightarrow 
2\alpha_j \le \bar{S_{T_k}} - S_{T_k} &(ii)
\end {align*}
The contradiction between  $(i)$  and  $(ii)$  warrants our proof. Now we're nearly done. Take a subset  $T_{half}$  of  $\{1,2,\dots,n\}$ , such that  $\sum_{i\in T_{half}} \alpha_i = \frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$ . Obviously  $\sum_{i\notin T_{half}} \alpha_i = \frac{S_z-S_x}{2}$  as well. So we may partition the stones in two groups,  $A$  and  $B$ , defined as follows:
\begin {align*}
A = \{a_i, d_i | i \in T_{half}\} \cup \{b_i, c_i | i \notin T_{half} \} 
B = \{b_i, c_i | i \in T_{half}\} \cup \{a_i, d_i | i \notin T_{half} \}
\end {align*}
Each group has exactly two stones of each colour  $i$ . If we calculate the weight difference between the groups, we get:
\begin {align*}
S_A-S_B & = \sum_{i \in T_{half}} (a_i+d_i) + \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} (b_i+c_i) - \sum_{i \in T_{half}} (b_i+c_i) - \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} (a_i+d_i) 
&= \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} {(b_i+c_i)-(a_i+d_i)} + \sum_{i \in T_{half}} {(b_i+c_i) - (a_i+d_i)} 
&= \sum_{i \notin T_{half}} \alpha_i - \sum_{i \in T_{half}} \alpha_i 
&= \frac{S_z-S_x}{2} - \frac{S_z-S_x}{2} 
&= 0 
\end {align*}
And that solves the problem.  $\hfill \blacksquare$

Let \(D\) be the maximum distance between two points in \(\mathcal S\). Let these two points be \(A\) and \(B\), and let \(\ell\) be the line \(AB\). If we consider the projections of the points in \(\mathcal S\) onto \(\ell\), let \(\delta\) be the maximum distance between two adjacent projections. I contend \(\delta\ge O(n^{-1/3})\). It will then suffice to take the perpendicular bisector of these two projections.**Case 1: \(D\ge O(n^{2/3})\).** There are \(n\) projections on a segment of length \(D\ge O(n^{2/3})\), so some two adjacent projections are \[\delta\ge\frac Dn\ge\frac{O(n^{2/3})}n=O(n^{-1/3})\] apart, as desired.**Case 2: \(D\le O(n^{2/3})\).** Consider the disks \(\Gamma_A\) and \(\Gamma_B\) centered at \(A\) and \(B\) with radii \(D\). Then all the points in \(\mathcal S\) lie in \(\Gamma_A\cap\Gamma_B\).

Consider a chord \(XY\) of \(\Gamma_B\) a distance of \(1/2\) away from \(A\), and consider the segment \(\mathcal R\) of \(\Gamma_B\) bounded by \(\overline{XY}\) and containing \(A\).

