<span style="color:red">**Claim:**</span>      \(\mathcal R\) contains at most \(O(\sqrt D)\) points.

*Proof.*      The Pythagorean theorem gives \(XY=O(\sqrt D)\).     Since points in \(\mathcal R\) are at least 1 apart     and the width of \(\mathcal R\) is \(\frac12\),     their projections onto \(XY\) must be at least \(\frac{\sqrt3}2\) apart,     implying there are at most \(O(\sqrt D)\) points in \(\mathcal R\). \(\blacksquare\)

The projections of the points in \(\mathcal R\) onto \(\ell\) cover a segment of length at most \(\frac12\), so some two adjacent projections are \[\delta\ge\frac{1/2}{\sqrt D}\ge\frac1{O(n^{2/3})}=O(n^{-1/3})\] apart as desired.

