<details><summary>My solution</summary>We denote by  $P(n)$  the required inequality, and we will solve the problem by strong induction on  $n>0$ .
*Induction Base.* Case  $n=1$  is trivial. For the case  $n=2$  let  $x_1=x, x_2=y$ , and  $P(2)$  reduces to  $\sqrt{2|x|}+\sqrt{2|y|}+2\sqrt{|x+y|}\ge 2\sqrt{|x-y|}$ . 
If  $\mathrm{sgn}(x)=\mathrm{sgn}(y)$ , the inequality is trivial.
Otherwise, assume that  $x\ge 0,\; y\le 0$  and let  $\{x’, y’\}=\{|x|, |y|\}$  such that  $x’\ge y’$ , and the inequality reduces to
\begin{align*}
&\sqrt{2x’}+\sqrt{2y’}+2\sqrt{x’-y’}\ge 2\sqrt{x’+y’}
&\Leftrightarrow (2x’+2y’+2\sqrt{4x’y’}+4(x’-y’)+2(\sqrt{2x’}+\sqrt{2y’})\sqrt{x’-y’}\ge 4x’+4y’
&\Leftrightarrow 4\sqrt{x’y’}+2x’\ge 6y’ 
\end{align*}
Which reduces to  $2\sqrt{x’y’}+x’\ge 3y’$ , true because  $x’\ge y’$  and  $2\sqrt{x’y’}\ge 2y’$ .
*Induction Step.*  $(1,2, \ldots, n+1)\mapsto (n+2)$ . Let  $n+2=m$ . **1.** First we consider the case when  $\exists i’,j’=\overline{1,m}$  such that  $x_{i’}+x_{j’}=0$ . 
If  $i’=j’$ , the problem reduces to  $P(n+1)$ .
If  $i’\ne j’$ , wLOG we can assume that  $i’=n+1$  and  $j’=n+2$ , and let  $x_{n+1}=x$ . Notice that
\begin{align*}
P(n+1)&\Leftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n+2}\sqrt{|x_i-x_j|}+\sum_{j=1}^{n+2} \left(\sqrt{|x-x_j|}+\sqrt{(-x)-x_j}\right) & \le \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n+2}\sqrt{|x_i+x_j|}+\sum_{j=1}^{n+2} \left(\sqrt{|x+x_j|}+\sqrt{(-x)+x_j}\right) 
&\Leftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^{n+2} \sqrt{|x_i-x_j|}\le \sum_{i=1}^n\sum_{j=1}^{n+2}\sqrt{|x_i+x_j|}
&\Leftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n\sqrt{|x_i-x_j|} +\sum_{i=1}^n \left(\sqrt{|x_i-x|}+\sqrt{|x_i-(-x)|}\right)
&\le \sum_{i=1}^n \sum_{j=1}^n\sqrt{|x_i+x_j|} +\sum_{i=1}^n \left(\sqrt{|x_i+x|}+\sqrt{|x_i+(-x)}\right)
&\Leftrightarrow P(n),\text{ true by induction hypothesis.}
\end{align*}**2.**Now we analyse the case  $\forall i,j=\overline{1,m}$ :  $x_i+x_j\ne 0$ .
Let  $R(x_1,\ldots, x_m)$  and  $L(x_1,\ldots,x_m)$  the RHS and the LHS of the inequality  $P(m)$ , respectively. 
Let  $\varepsilon =\frac12\min_{1\le i,j\le m} |x_i+x_j|>0$  and for each  $k\in \{1,2\}$  let
\begin{align*}
R_k(x_1,\ldots, x_m)&=\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m \sqrt{\left|(x_i+(-1)^k\varepsilon)+(x_j+(-1)^k\varepsilon)\right|}
&=\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m \sqrt{|x_i+x_j|+(-1)^k\cdot 2\varepsilon}.
\end{align*}
From this and Cauchy-Schwarz inequality it follows that
\begin{align*}
&R_1(x_1,\ldots, x_m) +R_2(x_1,\ldots, x_m)=\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m \left(\sqrt{|x_i+x_j|-2\varepsilon}+\sqrt{|x_i+x_j|+2\varepsilon}\right)
&\le \sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m \sqrt{2\left(\sqrt{|x_i+x_j|-2\varepsilon}^2+\sqrt{|x_i+x_j|+2\varepsilon}^2\right)}
&=2\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m \sqrt{x_i+x_j}=2R(x_1,\ldots, x_m),
\end{align*}
which implies that 
\[\forall x_1,\ldots, x_m\in\mathbb R\exists k\in \{1,2\}\text{ such that } R_k(x_1,\ldots, x_m)\le R(x_1,\ldots, x_m).\]
Let  $1\le p,q\le m$  such that  $|x_p+x+q|=\min_{1\le i,j\le m}|x_i+x_j|$  and  $\frac{x_p+x_q}{|x_p+x_q|}=\sigma\in \{-1,1\}$ , and for each  $i=\overline{1,m}$  let 
\[z_i=(-1)^{k+1}\sigma x_i,\; \varepsilon’=(-1)^{k+1}\varepsilon,\; y_i=z_i-\varepsilon’.\]
Therefore
\[y_p+y_q=(-1)^{k+1}(\sigma\cdot(x_p+x_q)-2\varepsilon)=(-1)^{k+1}(|x_p+x_q|-2\varepsilon)=0\;\;\; (2)\]
But 
\begin{align*}
L(y_1,\ldots, y_m)&=\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m \sqrt{|(z_i-\varepsilon’)-(z_j-\varepsilon’)|}
&=\sum_{i=1}^m\sum_{j=1}^m \sqrt{|z_i-z_j|}=L(z_1,\ldots, z_m).
\end{align*}
From this and  $\forall x_1’,\ldots, x_m’\in\mathbb R:\; L(x_1’,\ldots, x_m’)=L(-x_1’,\ldots, -x_m’)$  it follows that 
\[L(y_1,\ldots, y_m)=L(x_1,\ldots, x_m)\tag{3}\]
But for each  $\forall x_1’,\ldots, x_m’\in\mathbb R:\; R(x_1’,\ldots, x_m’)=R(-x_1’,\ldots, -x_m’)$ , which implies
\begin{align*}
R(x_1,\ldots, x_m)&=R(z_1,\ldots, z_m)\ge R_k(z_1,\ldots, z_m)
&=R(z_1+(-1)^k\varepsilon, \ldots, z_m+(-1)^k\varepsilon)=R(y_1,\ldots, y_m).
\end{align*}
From this and  $(3)$  it follows that  $P(n+2)$  reduces to
\[L(y_1,\ldots, y_m)\le R(y_1,\ldots, y_m).\]
By using  $(2)$ , we get that this inequality reduces to the case **1**. The induction is finished, and the problem is solved!
We have equality iff  $\{x_1,\ldots, x_n\}$  is symmetrically distributed across  $0$ .</details>

