It suffices to prove that
\[ AD + DT + XA = CD + DY + ZC \]
However,  by Pitot,  $AB - BC = AD - CD$ , and therefore it suffices to prove that
\[ BX + DT = DY +  BZ \Leftrightarrow  DT - DY = BZ - BX \ (\star) \]
By power of point, we have  $DT \cdot DC = DA \cdot DY \implies DT = \frac{DA}{DC} \cdot DY$  and  $BZ \cdot BC = BX \cdot BA \implies BZ = \frac{BA}{BC} \cdot BX$ . Substituting this to  $(\star)$ , and notice that  $DA - DC = BA - BC$  by Pitot, it suffices to prove that
\[ \frac{DY}{DC} = \frac{BX}{BC} \]
The easiest way to do this is by Law of Sine, which is
\[ \frac{DY}{DC} = \frac{\sin \angle DCY}{\sin \angle DYC} = \frac{\sin \angle TCY}{\sin \angle AYC} = \frac{\sin \angle XCB}{\sin \angle AXC} = \frac{\sin \angle BCX}{\sin \angle BXC} = \frac{BX}{BC} \]

