By Pitot we must have that  $AD+BC=CD+AB$ , thus we have that  $AD=CD+AB-BC$ , then our relation is translated into the following:  $$ BX+DT+TX=BZ+DY+YZ $$  By PoP we must have that:  $$ AD.DY=CD.DT \; \; \text{and} \; \; BA.BX=BC.BZ $$  Then we have that:  $$ DT=\frac{AD}{DC}.DY \; \; \text{and} \; \; BX=\frac{BC}{BA}.BZ $$  this implies that:  $$ BX-BZ = BZ\left( \frac{BC-BA}{BA} \right) \; \; \text{and} \; \; DT-DY=DY\left( \frac{AD-CD}{CD} \right) $$  By Pitot we must have that  $BC-BA=DC-DA$ , thus we must show that:  $$ YZ-TX \stackrel{?}{=}(AD-CD)\left( \frac{BZ}{BA} - \frac{DY}{DC} \right) $$  Now let's say that  $\angle A = \alpha$ ,  $\angle C = \gamma$  and let  $\angle D = \delta$ ,where  $\gamma > \alpha$ .
Simple angle chase gives us that:  $$ \angle AIC = 360-\delta-\frac{1}{2}\alpha-\frac{1}{2}\gamma $$    $$ \angle AYC = \delta + \frac{1}{2}\alpha + \frac{1}{2}\gamma-180 $$    $$ \angle YCZ = \frac{1}{2}(\gamma - \alpha) $$  Then we get that:  $$ \frac{DY}{DC}=\frac{\sin \frac{1}{2}(\gamma+\alpha)}{\sin \delta+\frac{1}{2}\alpha + \frac{1}{2}\gamma}=\frac{BZ}{BA} $$  Thus we need to show that  $XT=YZ$ .

But this holds, since:  $$ \angle XTY = 180-\angle XYZ = \frac{1}{2}(\alpha + \gamma) \; \; \text{and} \; \; \angle YZT = 180-\angle YXT = 180-\angle YCT = \gamma - \frac{1}{2}(\gamma - \alpha) = \frac{1}{2}(\gamma + \alpha) $$  thus  $XYZT$  is a isosceles trapezoid, thus  $XT=YZ$ , proving that:  $$ AD+DT+TX+XA=CD+DY+YZ+ZC $$

