We begin with the following claim:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span>  $TX=YZ$ .

*Proof.* It suffices to show that the measures of arc  $\widehat{TX}$  and  $\widehat{YZ}$  in  $(AIC)$  are equal. Notice that   $$ \angle B = \frac{\widehat{XZ}-\widehat{AC}}2, \ \ \angle D = \frac{\widehat{AC}+\widehat{YT}}2. $$   Denote  $a = 180^{\circ} - \angle A$  and cyclic permutations for simplicity. Compute   $$ b-d = \frac{\widehat{YT}-\widehat{XZ} + 2\widehat{AC}}2. $$   However, we also have   $$ \widehat{AC} = 360^{\circ} - 2 \angle AIC = 360^{\circ} - (a+c+2d) = b-d, $$   implying that we must have  $\widehat{YT} = \widehat{XZ}$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Therefore, it suffices to show   $$ AD+DT+XA = CD+DY+ZC \iff AB+DT+XA = BC+DY+ZC \iff BX+DT=BZ+DY $$   by Pitot. By Power of a Point, 
\begin{align*}
BX+DT&=BZ+DY 
\iff BX+DT &= \frac{BX \cdot AB}{BC} + \frac{TD \cdot CD}{AD} 
\iff BX \cdot BC \cdot AD + DT \cdot BC \cdot AD &= BX \cdot AB \cdot AD + TD \cdot CD \cdot BC 
\iff BX \cdot AD \cdot (BC-AB) &= DT \cdot BC \cdot (CD-AD) 
\iff BX \cdot AD &= DT \cdot BC 
\iff \frac{BX}{BC} = \frac{DT}{AD},
\end{align*}
where the second-to-last step follows by Pitot. But by the Law of Sines,   $$ \frac{BX}{BC} = \frac{\sin \angle BCX}{\sin \angle BXC} = \frac{\sin \angle TAD}{\sin \angle ATD} = \frac{DT}{AD} $$   because   $$ \angle TAD + \angle BCX = \angle TAY + \angle BCX = \angle XCZ + \angle BCX = 180^{\circ} $$   due to isosceles trapezoid  $TXYZ$ . This finishes the problem.  $\square$

