<details><summary>Trig</summary><blockquote>Let  $\Gamma$  be a circle with centre  $I$ , and  $A B C D$  a convex quadrilateral such that each of the segments  $A B, B C, C D$  and  $D A$  is tangent to  $\Gamma$ . Let  $\Omega$  be the circumcircle of the triangle  $A I C$ . The extension of  $B A$  beyond  $A$  meets  $\Omega$  at  $X$ , and the extension of  $B C$  beyond  $C$  meets  $\Omega$  at  $Z$ . The extensions of  $A D$  and  $C D$  beyond  $D$  meet  $\Omega$  at  $Y$  and  $T$ , respectively. Prove that \[A D+D T+T X+X A=C D+D Y+Y Z+Z C.\]

*Proposed by Dominik Burek, Poland and Tomasz Ciesla, Poland*</blockquote>
By Pitot's theorem on quadrilateral  $ABCD$ ,the problem condition becomes \[A B+D T+T X+X A=C B +D Y+Y Z+Z C.\]
<span style="color:#000">**Claim :**</span>  $TX=YZ$ Note that,
\begin{align*}&\angle IXY=\angle IAY=\angle IAB=180-\angle IAX=\angle IYX \implies IX=IY \rightarrow (1)
&\angle IZT=\angle ICT=\angle ICB=180-\angle ICZ=\angle ITZ \implies IZ=IT \rightarrow (2) \end{align*}
Combining  $(1),(2)$  we have that  $XYZT$  is an isosceles trapezium  $\implies TX=YZ $     $\blacksquare$ So the condition becomes \[A B+D T+X A=C B +D Y+Z C.\]  $\implies$  \[B X+D T=B Z+D Y.\]
Now , $\angle ICX=\angle TYX=\angle IXY=\angle IAD=\frac{\angle A}{2} \implies \angle XCB=\frac{\angle A+\angle C}{2}$ And as  $\angle CBX=\angle B$  we have  $\angle BXC=180-\angle B-\frac{\angle A+\angle C}{2}=\frac{\angle D-\angle B}{2}=y$ Also assume that  $\frac{\angle B+\angle D}{2}=x$ .So applying LoS on  $\triangle BXC$  gives,  $$ \frac{BX}{BC}=\frac{\sin \frac{\angle A+\angle C}{2}}{\sin y}=\frac{\sin(180-\frac{\angle B+\angle D}{2})}{\sin y}=\frac{\sin(180-x)}{\sin y}=\frac{\sin x}{\sin y} $$  Also by Power of Point,  $BA.BX=BC.BZ \implies \frac{BX}{BC}=\frac{BZ}{BA} \implies \frac{BX}{BC}=\frac{BZ}{BA}=\frac{\sin x}{\sin y}$ Now,
\begin{align*} \angle TCX &=\angle DCX 
 &=\angle TCD-\angle ICX 
&=\frac{\angle C-\angle A}{2} 
\implies \angle TAX &=\angle TCX=\frac{\angle C-\angle A}{2}
\implies \angle TAD &=180-\angle A-\frac{\angle C-\angle A}{2}
&=180-\frac{\angle A+\angle C}{2}=x \end{align*}
and also  $\angle ATD=\angle ATC=\angle AXC=y$  and by Power of Point  $DA.DY=DT.DC \implies \frac{DT}{DA}=\frac{DY}{DC}$ LoS on  $\triangle ATD$  gives,  $$ \frac{DY}{DC}=\frac{DT}{DA}=\frac{\sin \angle TAD}{\sin \angle ATD}=\frac{\sin x}{\sin y}=\frac{BX}{BC}=\frac{BZ}{BA} $$  So we have,  $$ BX+DT=(AD+BC)\frac{\sin x}{\sin y} \stackrel{Pitot}=(AC+BD)\frac{\sin x}{\sin y}=BZ+DY \text{  } \blacksquare  $$</details>

