My Same Solution of the Problem by Complex Number.

 Let  $S$  be the origin. Note that  $\triangle SBC$  and  $\triangle SDE$  are directly similar with scale factor  $1$ , so there exists some  $\omega$  with  $|\omega|=1$  so that  $d=b\omega$  and  $e=c\omega$ . Also, let  $\alpha$  be such that  $|\alpha|=1$  and  $$ \frac{a-b}{(b-t)\alpha},\frac{(a-e)\alpha}{e-t}\in\mathbb R, $$  which encapsulates the fact that  $\angle ABS=\angle SEA$ . We will show that  $$ \frac{\frac{p-q}{p-r}}{\frac{s-q}{s-r}}\in\mathbb R. $$  For complex numbers  $x,y\neq 0$ , write  $x\sim y$  if and only if  $x/y\in\mathbb R$ . By various collinearities,  $p-r\sim c-d$ ,  $s-r\sim a-e$ , and  $p-q\sim a-b$ . Also, by the above,  $(a-b)(a-e)\sim (b-t)\alpha\frac{e-t}{\alpha}=be=cd$ . So, we need only show that  $$ \frac{(s-q)(c-d)}{cd}\in\mathbb R. $$  We compute  $q$  and  $s$ . The definition of  $Q$  gives  $$ \frac{q-b}{b\alpha},\frac qc\in\mathbb R, $$  so  $$ (q-b)\overline b\overline\alpha=(\overline q-\overline b)b\alpha\implies q\overline b-\overline q(b\alpha^2)=b\overline b(1-\alpha^2) $$  and  $q\overline c=\overline qc$ ; solving gives  $$ q(\overline bc-b\overline c\alpha^2)=q\overline bc-\overline q(b\alpha^2)c=bc(1-\alpha^2)\overline b $$  and so  $$ q=\frac{bc(1-\alpha^2)\overline b}{\overline bc-b\overline c\alpha^2}. $$  Similarly,  $$ s=\frac{de(1-\alpha^{-2})\overline e}{\overline ed-e\overline d\alpha^{-2}}. $$  Combining these and writing in terms of  $c$  and  $d$ ,
\begin{align*}
s-q
&=\frac{de(1-\alpha^{-2})\overline e}{\overline ed-e\overline d\alpha^{-2}}-\frac{bc(1-\alpha^2)\overline b}{\overline bc-b\overline c\alpha^2}
&=\frac{dc(1-\alpha^{-2})\overline c}{\overline cd\omega^{-1}-c\omega\overline d\alpha^{-2}}-\frac{dc(1-\alpha^2)\overline d}{\overline dc\omega-d\overline c\alpha^2\omega^{-1}}
&=\frac{cd(1-\alpha^2)\overline c\omega-cd(1-\alpha^2)\overline d\omega}{c\overline d\omega^2-\overline cd\alpha^2}
&=\frac{cd(1-\alpha^2)\omega(\overline{c-d})}{c\overline d\omega^2-\overline cd\alpha^2}.
\end{align*}
So, we need to show that  $$ \frac{(1-\alpha^2)\omega(\overline{c-d})(c-d)}{c\overline d\omega^2-\overline cd\alpha^2} $$  is real. The numerator  $N$  satisfies  $$ \frac{N}{\overline N}=\frac{(1-\alpha^2)\omega}{\overline{(1-\alpha^2)\omega}}=-\alpha^2\omega^2 $$  and the denominator  $D$  satisfies  $$ \frac{D}{\overline D}=\frac{c\overline d\omega^2-\overline cd\alpha^2}{\overline cd\omega^{-2}-c\overline d\alpha^{-2}}=\alpha^2\omega^2\frac{c\overline d\omega^2-\overline cd\alpha^2}{\overline cd\alpha^2-c\overline d\omega^2}=-\alpha^2\omega^2, $$  so  $\frac{N/D}{\overline{N/D}}=1$  as desired.

