We split into cases.



- If  $b \geq 2p$ , note that  $v_{p} (b!) \geq 2$ , so  $v_{p} (b! + p) = 1$ . Since  $v_{p} \left( a^p \right)$  must be a multiple of  $p$ , this is a contradiction.
- If  $2p > b \geq p$ , note that   $$ b! \leq (2p-1)! \leq \left( 1 \cdot (2p-1) \right) \cdot \left( 2 \cdot (2p-2) \right) \dots \cdot \left( (p-1) \cdot (p+1)\right) \cdot p < p^{2p-1}. $$  So,  $a^p = b! + p < p^{2p-1} + p < p^{2p}$ , so  $a < p^2$ . Furthermore,  $a$  must be a multiple of  $p$ . Notice that since  $b!$  is a multiple of every number from  $2$  to  $p-1$ ,  $b! + p$  is not a multiple of any of these numbers. Therefore,  $a$  cannot be a multiple of any number from  $2$  to  $p-1$ , so  $a=p$ . We now need to solve  $p^p - p = b!$ . We resort to madness; we will prove that  $p^p - p \not\equiv 0 \pmod{ p+2}$ . Let  $p+2 = x$ ; clearly,  $p \neq 2$ , so let's assume  $x$  is an odd greater than or equal to  $5$ . We have that
\begin{align*}
& (x-2)^{x-2} - x+2 
\equiv ~ & \text{stuff} - 2^{x-2} - x + 2 
\equiv ~ & 2-2^{x-2} 
\equiv ~ & 2(1-2^{x-3}) 
\equiv ~ & 1 - 2^{x-3} 
\equiv ~ & 2^{x-1} - 2^{x-3} 
\equiv ~ & 3 \cdot 2^{x-3} 
\not\equiv ~ & 0 \pmod{x}.
\end{align*}
So we only must check  $b = p$  and  $b = p+1$ . It is now obvious by some basic bounding that our only solutions in this case are  $(a,b,p) = \boxed{(2,2,2)}$  and  $\boxed{(3,4,3)}$ .
- If  $b < p$ , it follows that  $a < b$ , except for when  $p=2$ . If  $p=2$ , then  $b=1$  and there are no solutions clearly. So, we may assume  $a<b$ . But then,  $b!$  is a multiple of  $a$ , and  $p$  isn't, so  $b! + p$  cannot be a multiple of  $a$  - this is a contradiction.

Solved with hints from **IAmTheHazard**, **PikaPika007** and **DolphinDay**.

The only solutions are  $\boxed{(2, 2, 2)}$  and  $\boxed{(3, 4, 3)}$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:</span>**  $p \leq b < 2p$ .
*Proof.* For the upper bound assume  $b \geq 2p$ . Then  $p \mid b! + p$  and hence  $p \mid a$ . However then  $\nu_p(a^p) \geq p$  yet \[\nu_p(b! + p) = \min\{\nu_p(b!) + \nu_p(p) \} = 1\] as  $\nu_p(b!) \neq \nu_p(p)$ . Then  $\nu_p(\text{RHS}) > \nu_p(\text{LHS})$ , contradiction. 

For the lower bound  assume that  $b < p$ . Clearly then  $b! < b^p$ . However then we require,
\begin{align*}
a^p - b^p &= p
\end{align*}
We claim that  $a^p - b^p > p$  always holds as long as  $a > b$ . Note that the tightest bound we have is when  $a = b + 1$ . This gives,
\begin{align*}
(b+1)^p - b^p &> b^p + pb^{p-1} - b^p
&> pb^{p-1}
&> p
\end{align*}
Thus we must have the desired bounds.  $\square$ **<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>**  $a = p$ .
*Proof.* Consider the smallest prime divisor of  $a$ , say  $q$ . Note that  $p \mid a$  and  $b \geq p$ , so  $b \geq q$ . Then we find that,
\begin{align*}
p \equiv 0 \pmod{q}
\end{align*}
Then clearly  $p$  is the smallest prime divisor of  $a$ . Thus let  $a = pk$ . However note that,
\begin{align*}
(pk)^p - p &= b!
(pk)^p - p &< (2p)!
\end{align*}
However now noting that  $k > p+1$  as  $p$  is the smallest prime divisor of  $a$ , we must have,
\begin{align*}
(p(p+2))^p - p \leq (pk)^p - p
\end{align*}
Then we find,
\begin{align*}
(p(p+2))^p - p < (2p)!
\end{align*}
However from AM-GM we may conclude,
\begin{align*}
(2p)! &\leq \left(\frac{1+2+\dots + 2p}{2p} \right)^{2p}
&= \left(\frac{2p+1}{2} \right)^{2p}
&= \left( \frac{4p^2 + 4p + 1}{4} \right)^p
\end{align*}
Then we have,
\begin{align*}
(p^2 + 2p)^p - p &< \left(p^2 + p + \frac{1}{4} \right)^p
\left(p^2 + 2p - p^2 - p - \frac{1}{4} \right)(\dots) &< p
\left(p - \frac{1}{4} \right)(\dots) &< p
\end{align*}
which can easily be observed to fail. Thus  $k = 1$  and in fact  $a = p$ .  $\square$ Now we have reduced the desired to,
\begin{align*}
p^p &= b! + p
b! &= p^p - p
b! &= p(p^{p-1} - 1)
\end{align*}
Now note that  $\nu_2(p^{p-1} - 1) = 2\nu_2(p - 1) + \nu_2(p + 1) - 1 \leq 2\log_2(p-1)$ .  However noting that  $\nu_2(p!) \geq p - \log_2(p+1)$  we find that we require,
\begin{align*}
2\log_2(p-1) &> p - \log_2(p+1)
\iff p &< 10
\end{align*}
Now we may easily finish with a finite case check of  $p = 2$ ,  $p = 3$ ,  $p = 5$  and  $p = 7$ .

**Answer:**  $2n(n-1) + 1$ .**Solution.** Given a pair of adjacent cells, we can always continue walking "downhill" until we reach a valley, so any pair of adjacent cells is the highest pair in at least one uphill path. There are  $2n(n-1)$  such pairs, and the cell containing  $1$  alone also constitutes an uphill path, so there must be at least  $2n(n-1) + 1$  uphill paths.

A construction obtaining exactly  $2n(n-1) + 1$  is given as follows. We first construct a tree of  $T$  cells such that every cell not in the tree is only adjacent to cells in the tree. A diagram of such a tree for  $n=9$  is given below; this readily generalizes to any multiple of  $3$ , and to non-multiples of  $3$  by removing the leftmost and/or the rightmost column.  We place the numbers from  $1$  to  $T$  as follows:  $1$  is placed in an arbitrary cell of the tree, and then each number is placed adjacent to exactly one number that was already placed. (Using a depth-first-search order works, for example.) The numbers  $T+1$  to  $n^2$  are placed arbitrarily in the remaining cells.

