We claim that the answer is all positive integers of the form  $p^m$  for some prime  $p$  and  $m\ge 2$ . These clearly all work since for all  $1\le i \le m+1$  ,  $d_i=p^{i-1}$ . Thus, 
\[d_i=p_{i-1}\mid p_i + p_{i+1}=d_{i+1}+d_{i+2}\]
quite clearly.

Now, we show that no other composite  $n$  work. Say  $n$  has atleast two distinct primes divisors, of which the smallest two are  $p<q$ . Note that we have  $d_i = p^{i-1}$  for all  $1\le i \le r$  for some  $r \ge 2$  and  $d_{r+1}=q$  as the divisors are arranged in increasing order. Now, if  $n$  satisfies the desired condition we must have, 
\begin{align*}
d_{m-r} &\mid d_{m-r+1} + d_{m-r+2}
\frac{n}{d_{r+1}} & \mid \frac{n}{d_r} + \frac{n}{d_{r-1}}
\frac{n}{q} & \mid \frac{n}{p^{r-1}} + \frac{n}{p^{r-2}}
p^{r-1} & \mid q + pq
p &\mid q+pq
p& \mid q
\end{align*}
which is a very clear contradiction since  $q>p$  are both primes. Thus, there are no such  $n$  of this form, which finishes the proof.

I claim that the answer is  $n = p^x$ , for a prime  $p$  and any  $x \in \mathbb{Z^{+}}$ . We first start off by showing that this indeed works.

Note that when  $n = p^x$  we have that  $d_{i} = p^{i-1}$  for any  $i = $  { $1,2\dots ,x+1$ }. Hence we have that,  $d_{i} = p^{i_1}$ ,   $d_{i+1} = p^{i}$  and  $d_{i+2} = p^{i+1}$ .
Therefore,
\begin{align*}
d_{i+1} + d_{i+2} &= p^{i-1}(p+p^2) 
\implies d_{i} &\mid d_{i+1} + d_{i+2} 
\end{align*}

Now let's prove that all other  $n$  doesn't work.
Take  $p$  and  $q$  to be the smallest prime divisors of  $n$ , with  $p < q$ . Note that for some  $m$ ,  $d_{i} = p^{i -1}$  for  $1 \leq i \leq m$ , and  $d_{m+1} = q$ . Now we have that,
\begin{align*}
d_{k-m} &\mid d_{k-m+1} + d_{k-m+2} 

\frac{n}{d_{m+1}} &\mid \frac{n}{d_{m}} + \frac{n}{d_{m-1}} 

\frac{n}{q} &\mid \frac{n}{p^{m-1}} + \frac{n}{p^{m-2}} 

\implies p^{m-1} &\mid q + pq 
\implies p &\mid q
\end{align*}
which is clearly a contradiction. Therefore we can conclude that  $n$  can't have two or more prime factors. Hence  $n = p^{x}$  is the only solution.

We claim that such  $P$  exists if and only if  $\{a_n\}$  is a nondecreasing arithmetic sequence. If  $\{a_n\}$  is a nondecreasing arithmetic sequence, then letting  $d$  be its common difference, we can take \[P(x)=(x+d)(x+2d)\dots (x+kd)\]

We now show that if such  $P$  exists,  $\{a_n\}$  is a nondecreasing arithmetic sequence. Suppose  $P(x)=x^k$ . Let  $m$  be the minimum value of a term in  $\{a_n\}$ . Then if  $a_n=m$ , we must also have \[a_{n+1}=a_{n+2}=\dots=a_{n+k}=m\] and if  $n>1$ ,  $a_{n-1}=m$ . Then  $\{a_n\}$  must be constant.

Now suppose  $P(x)\neq x^k$ . Let  $S$  be the set of  $n$  for which \[a_n\leq\min\{a_{n+1},a_{n+2},\dots,a_{n+k}\}\] We note that if  $n\in S$  for some  $n>1$ , then
\begin{align*}
    &P(a_{n-1})=a_na_{n+1}\dots a_{n+k-1}
    \implies &P(a_{n-1})\leq a_{n+1}a_{n+2}\dots a_{n+k}=P(a_n)
    \implies &a_{n-1}\leq a_n
    \implies &n-1\in S
\end{align*}
Now suppose FTSOC  $S$  is finite. Then for all  $n>\max\{S\}$ , at least one of  $a_{n+1},a_{n+2},\dots,a_{n+k}$  is less than  $a_n$ . Then  $\{a_n\}$  has a decreasing subsequence, which is impossible. Then  $S$  is infinite and we must have  $S=\mathbb Z^+$ .

It follows that  $\{a_n\}$  is nondecreasing. Suppose FTSOC  $\{a_n\}$  is bounded; then it is eventually some constant  $M$ . However, we then have  $P(M)=M^k$  which is obviously impossible, and so  $a_n$  is unbounded. Now pick some large  $a_n$ . We have
\begin{align*}
    &P(a_n)
    =&a_{n+1}a_{n+2}\dots a_{n+k}
    =&(a_n+(a_{n+1}-a_n))(a_n+(a_{n+2}-a_n))\dots (a_n+(a_{n+k}-a_n))
    =&\sum_{i=0}^k \sigma_{k-i}(a_{n+1}-a_n,a_{n+2}-a_n,\dots,a_{n+k}-a_n)a_n^i
\end{align*}
Let \[t_i=\sigma_{k-i}(a_{n+1}-a_n,a_{n+2}-a_n,\dots,a_{n+k}-a_n)\] and \[Q(x)=x^k+t_{k-1}x^{k-1}+\dots+t_0\] Note that each  $t_i$  is nonnegative. For sufficiently large  $a_n$ ,  $t_{k-1}>c_{k-1}$  would imply  $P(a_n)<Q(a_n)$ . Then  $t_{k-1}\leq c_{k-1}$ . Then there are finitely many possibilities for  $(a_{n+1}-a_n,a_{n+2}-a_n,\dots,a_{n+k}-a_n)$  and each  $t_i$  is upper bounded by some constant depending only on  $c_{k-1}$ . Suppose FTSOC  $P(x)\neq Q(x)$ , and pick the largest  $i$  for which  $c_i\neq t_i$ . For sufficiently large  $a_n$ , we would have that  $P(a_n)>Q(a_n)$  if  $c_i>t_i$  and  $P(a_n)<Q(a_n)$  if  $c_i<t_i$ . Then  $P(x)=Q(x)$  for sufficiently large  $n$ . Fix some such  $n$ , and take \[\{d_1,d_2,\dots,d_k\}=\{a_{n+1}-a_n,a_{n+2}-a_n,\dots,a_{n+k}-a_n\}\] where both sides are taken as multisets. Then \[P(x)=(x+d_1)(x+d_2)\dots (x+d_k)\] Then performing this argument for any such  $n$  will gives that \[\{a_{n+1}-a_n,a_{n+2}-a_n,\dots,a_{n+k}-a_n\}=\{d_1,d_2,\dots,d_k\}\] for sufficiently large  $n$ . WLOG let  $d_1\leq d_2\leq \dots \leq d_k$ . Then we have \[(a_{n+1},a_{n+2},\dots,a_{n+k})=(a_n+d_1,a_n+d_2,\dots,a_n+d_k)\] Let  $d=d_1$ . Then from  $a_{n+1}=a_n+d$  it follows that  $d_i=id$ .

Then  $\{a_n\}$  is eventually arithmetic. Finally, we note that
\begin{align*}
    &(a_{n+1},a_{n+2},\dots,a_{n+k})=(a_n+d,a_n+2d,\dots,a_n+kd)
    \implies &P(a_{n-1})=a_n(a_n+d)\dots (a_n+(k-1)d)
    \implies &a_{n-1}=a_n-d
\end{align*}
It follows that  $\{a_n\}$  is an arithmetic sequence.

