It is clear that  $a_n$  is strictly increasing. We will show that  $a_{n+2} \geq a_n+3$  for all  $n$ . Suppose otherwise, so  $a_{n+1}=a_n+1$  and  $a_{n+2}=a_{n+1}+1$ . We have
\begin{align*}
(a_n+1)^2&=(x_1+\cdots+x_n+x_{n+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}+\frac{1}{x_{n+1}}\right)
&=\underbrace{(x_1+\cdots+x_n)\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}\right)}_{a_n^2}+x_{n+1}\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}\right)+\frac{1}{x_{n+1}}(x_1+\cdots+x_n)+1
\implies 2a_n&=x_{n+1}\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}\right)+\frac{1}{x_{n+1}}(x_1+\cdots+x_n)
\end{align*}
At this point, observe that the definition of  $a_m$  is homogeneous in  $x_1,\ldots,x_m$ , so WLOG assume that  $\tfrac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\tfrac{1}{x_n}=1$ , hence  $x_1+\cdots+x_n)=a_n^2$ . The final equation then becomes the quadratic  $x^2-2a_nx+a_n^2$ , implying  $x_{n+1}=a_n$ . But we also have
\begin{align*}
(a_n+2)^2&=(x_1+\cdots+x_n+x_{n+1}+x_{n+2})\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}+\frac{1}{x_{n+1}}+\frac{1}{x_{n+2}}\right)
&=(x_1+\cdots+x_n)\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}\right)+x_{n+1}\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}\right)+\frac{1}{x_{n+1}}(x_1+\cdots+x_n)
&\hphantom{=}+x_{n+2}\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\cdots+\frac{1}{x_n}\right)+\frac{1}{x_{n+2}}(x_1+\cdots+x_n)+\frac{x_{n+2}}{x_{n+1}}+\frac{x_{n+1}}{x_{n+2}}+2
&=a_n^2+2a_n+2+x_{n+2}+\frac{x_{n+2}}{a_n}+\frac{a_n^2}{x_{n+2}}+\frac{a_n}{x_{n+2}}
&=a_n^2+2a_n+2+(a_n+1)\left(\frac{x_{n+2}}{a_n}+\frac{a_n}{x_{n+2}}\right)
&>a_n^2+2a_n+2+2(a_n+1)=(a_n+2)^2,
\end{align*}
where the last inequality is from AM-GM, noting that  $x_{n+2}=a_n=x_{n+1}$  is not allowed. But this is a contradiction, so we must have  $a_{n+2} \geq a_n+3$ . The conclusion clearly follows.  $\blacksquare$ **Remark:** Solving for  $x_{n+1}$  explicitly is not necessary, but it makes the correct algebraic manipulations blindingly obvious

