<details><summary>Solution</summary>We will prove a stronger claim, in fact.**<span style="color:#0ff">Main Claim:</span>** For all integers  $1\leq k\leq 1012$ , we have that   $$ a_{2k-1} \geq 3k-2. $$  **<span style="color:#0ff">Proof:</span>** We use induction. Note that for  $k = 1$ , we have that  $a_1 = \sqrt{x_1\cdot \frac{1}{x_1}} = 1 \geq 3\cdot 1 - 2 = 1$ , so our base case is established. Now, assume that for some  $1\leq k\leq 1011$ , we have that  $a_{2k-1} \geq 3k-2$ . Then, note that 
\begin{align*}
a_{2(k+1)-1} &= a_{2k+1} 
             &= \sqrt{(x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)} 
             &= \sqrt{(x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_{2k-1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k-1}}\right) + (x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k-1}}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)(x_1+x_2+\cdots + x_{2k-1}) + (x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)}.
\end{align*}
By the inductive hypothesis, we know that   $$ (x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_{2k-1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k-1}}\right) = a_{2k-1}^2 \geq (3k-2)^2. $$   By AM-GM, Cauchy-Schwarz, and the inductive hypothesis, we know that 
\begin{align*}
(x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k-1}}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)(x_1+x_2+\cdots + x_{2k-1}) &\geq 2\sqrt{(x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)(x_1+x_2+\cdots + x_{2k-1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k-1}}\right)}  &= 2\sqrt{(x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)a_{2k-1}^2}  &\geq 2\sqrt{4a_{2k-1}^2}  &= 4a_{2k-1}  &= 4(3k-2).
\end{align*}
Now, by Cauchy-Schwarz, we know that   $$ (x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right) \geq 4. $$   However, if equality were to be held, then we would need   $$ x_{2k}^2 = \frac{x_{2k}}{\frac{1}{x_{2k}}} = \frac{x_{2k+1}}{\frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}} = x_{2k+1}^2, $$   so  $x_{2k} = x_{2k+1}$  since the  $x_i$  are positive reals, but this contradicts the fact that  $x_1, x_2, \cdots, x_{2023}$  are pairwise distinct. Thus, equality cannot be held, so we have that   $$ (x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right) > 4. $$   Thus, putting this all together, we have that 
\begin{align*}
a_{2(k+1)-1} &= \sqrt{(x_1 + x_2 + \cdots + x_{2k-1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k-1}}\right) + (x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_1} + \frac{1}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{1}{x_{2k-1}}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)(x_1+x_2+\cdots + x_{2k-1}) + (x_{2k} + x_{2k+1})\left(\frac{1}{x_{2k}} + \frac{1}{x_{2k+1}}\right)}  &> \sqrt{(3k-2)^2 + 4(3k-2) + 4}  &= \sqrt{((3k-2) + 2)^2} &= 3k,
\end{align*}
so  $a_{2(k+1)-1} > 3k$ , so since  $a_{2(k+1)-1}$  is an integer, it follows that  $a_{2(k+1) - 1} \geq 3k+1 = 3(k+1) - 2$ , as desired. Thus, the inductive step is complete and so the claim is true.  $\square$ Thus, setting  $k = 1012$  in the statement of the main claim, we have that   $$ a_{2023} = a_{2\cdot 1012 - 1} \geq 3\cdot 1012 - 2 = 3034, $$   as desired. Thus, we have proved the given bound and so we are done.  $\blacksquare$</details>

