By induction hypothesis, 
\[\sum_{j=1}^{i+1} 2^{-y'_j} \leq \sum_{j=1}^{a} 2^{-x_j} + 2^{-M}+ \sum_{j=a+2}^{i} 2^{-x_{j-1}}\leq 1.\]

However the sum we would like to compute has the red circle, which means that its actual label should be  $y_i = y'_i+1$  since it counts that circle in the path as well. Hence, the actual sum must be  $1 - 2^{-y_i} \leq 1 - 2^{-N}$  as desired.

Now, on the last row, we can choose the cell labeled with  $x_j$  such that  $\frac{1}{2^{x_j}} < \frac{1}{n}\implies 2^{x_j} > n \geq 2^{k-1}$ , implying the result. Otherwise, if we cannot choose such index  $j$ , then  $\sum_{j=1}^n 2^{-x_j} \geq \frac{n}{n} = 1,$  contradiction.

The answer is  $\lfloor \log_2 n\rfloor+1$ . To show that this is maximal, we use the same construction of forming "slope -1/2 lines", with each ninja path and "line" only possibly intersecting once. We now show that this is always attainable. Clearly, it suffices to show that we can always find a ninja path containing at least  $n+1$  red circles on a Japanese triangle with side length  $2^n$ .

Define the *power* of a circle as follows: the topmost circle (which is red) has power  $1$ , and the power of any other circle is  $1$  plus the maximum power across every **red** circle above it which also shares some ninja path with the circle itself, and let the power of a row be the sum of the powers of its circles. Define  $p_{i,j}$  to be the power of the  $j$ -th circle from the left on row  $i$ , and let  $P_i=p_{i,1}+\cdots+p_{i,i}$ . It evidently suffices to show that some circle has power at least  $n+1$ . To that end, I claim row  $2^n$  has power at least  $n2^n+1$ . This is by induction on  $n$ , with the base case of  $n=0$  being trivial. Now suppose that on row  $2^n$ , the sum of circle powers is at least  $n2^n+1$ , so there also exists some circle on that row with power at least  $n+1$ . We now prove a general claim**<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>** We have  $P_{i+1} \geq P_i+\max_{j}a_{i,j}+1$ .
<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span> In general we should have the recursion  $$ a_{i+1,j}=\max\{a_{i,j},a_{i,j-1}\}+r_{i+1,j}, $$  where  $a_{i,-1}=a_{i,i+1}=0$  and  $r_{i+1,j}$  is an indicator variable equal to  $1$  if the corresponding circle is red and  $0$  otherwise. Pick some  $1 \leq k \leq i$  such that  $a_{i,k}$  is maximal. Then we have
\begin{align*}
a_{i+1,0} &\geq a_{i,0}+r_{i+1,0}
a_{i+1,1} &\geq a_{i,1}+r_{i+1,1}
&\vdots
a_{i+1,k} &\geq a_{i,k}+r_{i+1,k}
a_{i+1,k+1} &\geq a_{i,k}+r_{i+1,k+1}
a_{i+1,k+2} &\geq a_{i,k+1}+r_{i+1,k+2}
&\vdots
a_{i+1,i+1} &\geq a_{i,i}+r_{i+1,i+1}.
\end{align*}
Since exactly one  $r_{i+1,j}$  is  $1$ , summing these inequalities shows that  $P_{i+1} \geq P_i+a_{i,k}+1=P_i+\max_j a_{i,j}+1$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Returning to the main problem, by inductive hypothesis we have  $\max_j a_{2^n+i,j} \geq n+1$  for all  $i \geq 0$  (clearly  $\max_j a_{x,j}$  is nondecreasing in  $x$ ). Therefore,  $$ P_{2^{n+1}}\geq P_{2^n}+(n+1)2^n+2^n=(2n+2)2^n+1=(n+1)2^{n+1}+1, $$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ **Remark:** A very nice algebra problem!

