This works because any ninja path must pass at most one red circle from row  $1$ , row  $2-3$ , row  $4-7$ ,  $\dots$ . It generalizes immediately to  $n=2^k-1$  using  $k$  levels of size  $1$ ,  $2$ ,  $4$ ,  $\dots$ ,  $2^{k-1}$ .**Lower Bound**

It suffices to prove for  $n=2^k$  that there is a ninja path passing through  $k+1$  red circles. For each red circle, emit a [i]cone* by drawing two lines parallel to the two sides of the equilateral triangle from that circle. Clearly, for two red circles  $a$ ,  $b$ , there is a ninja path from  $a$  to  $b$  if and only if the cone of  $b$  is completely contained in the cone of  $a$ .

Thus, if we create a poset of all the  $2^k$  red circles, where we designate  $a\prec b$  if and only if the cone of  $a$  is to the left of the cone of  $b$ . Then,  $a$  and  $b$  are incomparable iff one cone is contained within another. Combining with the above paragraph, a ninja path corresponds to antichain in this poset. Thus, assume that there is no antichain of size  $k+1$ . Then, by **Dilworth's theorem**, one can decompose this poset to  $k$  chains.

Let the chains be  $C_1, C_2,\dots,C_k$ , and for each  $i$ , let  $m_i$  be the minimum row number across all red circles in  $C_i$ . WLOG, assume  $m_1 < m_2 < \dots <m_k$ . We prove the following two claims.
-----
<span style="color:red">**Claim:**</span>  $\lvert C_i\rvert \leq m_i$ .

*Proof.* Suppose that the element in the  $m_i$ -th row is the  $x$ -th circle. Then, there are at most  $x-1$  cones to the left and at most  $m_i-x$  cones to the right. Thus, there are at most  $(x-1) + (m_i-x) + 1 = m_i$  cones in total.  $\blacksquare$ -----
<span style="color:red">**Claim:**</span>  $m_i \leq m_1+m_2+\dots+m_{i-1}+1$ .

*Proof.* Consider the  $m_i-1$  red circles in the row  $1,2,\dots,m_i-1$ . These circles must be in one of the  $C_1,C_2,\dots,C_{i-1}$ , so by the previous claim, there are at most  $m_1+\dots+m_{i-1}$  of them. Thus,  $m_i-1 \leq m_1+\dots +m_{i-1}$ , as claimed.  $\blacksquare$ -----
By the second claim, we get that
\begin{align*}
m_1 &\leq 1 
m_2 &\leq 1+1=2 
m_3 &\leq (1+2)+1 = 4 
m_4 &\leq (1+2+4+1)+1 = 8 
&\vdots 
m_k &\leq 2^{k-1},
\end{align*}
so by the first claim, there are at most  $m_1+\dots+m_k = 2^k-1$  red circles, a contradiction.
-------
*Remark.* (Setting up the poset). There are two ways to set up the poset. One way is in above, where you get to use **Dilworth's theorem**. Another way is to set  $a\prec b$  if and only if the cone of  $a$  is completely contained in the cone of  $b$ . If you do it this way, you get to use **Mirsky's theoem** instead (because a path becomes a chain).

Here is a very ``local" way to prove achievability. 

It suffices to show a Japanese triangle of size  $2^k$  always has a ninja path containing at least  $k+1$  red circles. We prove this via induction with  $k=0$  vacuous. Say a circle  $b$  is *within vision* of  $a$  if there is a ninja path that passes through  $a$  and  $b$ , in that order.

By the inductive hypothesis, there is a smaller ninja path in the mini Japanese triangle of size  $2^{k-1}$  atop the original triangle which contains  $k$  red circles; say the last red circle of the path is circle  $c$ . If there is a red circle  $r$  within vision of  $c$ , then we are done: walk along the smaller ninja path to  $c$ , then from  $c$  to  $r$ .

[asy]
unitsize(4mm);

bool contains(int[] arr, int key) {
	for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; ++i) {
    	if (arr*== key) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

pair pt(int i, int j) {
	return 2*i*dir(240)+2*(j,0);
}

void japan(int[] l, int n, int[][] blnks) {
	for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    	for (int j = 0; j <= i; ++j) {
        	if (j == l[i]) {
            	filldraw(circle(pt(i,j),1),lightred,black);
            } else if (contains(blnks[i], j)) {
            	draw(circle(pt(i,j),1),black);
            }
        }
    }
}

int[] A = {0,0,2,2,4,1,0,6};
int[][] B = {{},{},{},{},{},{},{},{}};

japan(A, 8, B);
draw(pt(0,0)--pt(2,2)--pt(3,2),linewidth(1.5));
draw(pt(3,2)--pt(7,6),linewidth(1.5)+gray(0.5));
[/asy]

If there is no red circle within vision of  $c$ , we will [i]chop off* (ignore) any circle within vision of  $c$  or in the same row as  $c$ . We can safely move any circle in a row beneath  $c$  up one row without changing any properties of the remaining circles.

[asy]
unitsize(4mm);

bool notcontains(int[] arr, int key) {
	for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; ++i) {
    	if (arr*== key) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

pair pt(int i, int j, pair s) {
	return 2*i*dir(240)+2*(j,0)+s;
}

void japan(int[] l, int n, int[][] blnks, int[][] grays, pair s) {
	for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    	for (int j = 0; j <= i; ++j) {
        	if (!notcontains(grays[i], j)) {
            	draw(circle(pt(i,j,s),1),gray(0.8));
            } else if (j == l[i]) {
            	filldraw(circle(pt(i,j,s),1),lightred,black);
            } else if(notcontains(blnks[i], j)) {
            	draw(circle(pt(i,j,s),1),black);
            }
        }
    }
}

fill(circle(pt(3,2,(0,0)),1),lightred+white);

int[] A = {0,0,2,2,4,1,0,1};
int[][] B = {{},{},{},{},{},{},{},{}};
int[][] G = {{},{},{},{0,1,2,3},{2,3},{2,3,4},{3,4,5,2},{3,4,5,6,2}};

japan(A, 8, B, G, (0,0));

